('6020e987-74d8-404e-bee7-401d2baddfcb', 'Advanced Topics in Solid State Physics: Solid Mechanics', 'PHYS6100', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'F'),
('c2786358-0ec5-46ea-943e-fab55979f53b', 'Advanced Topics in Solid State Physics: Solid Mechanics', 'PHYS6100', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'W'),
('a3423642-af10-493b-b41b-5fbd2f86ef1f', 'Selected Topics in Applied Optical Physics', 'PHYS6170', 3.0, 'Topics may change from year to year. Typical subject material may be selected from: design of advanced optical components, instruments and systems, detectors and instruments, the principles of laser radar (lidar), the interaction of laser radiation with materials, optical communication systems, advanced instrumentation for astronomy and space science.', 'GS', 'F'),
('03a1795d-3c48-40ab-ac0c-622889cc4754', 'Selected Topics in Applied Optical Physics', 'PHYS6170', 3.0, 'Topics may change from year to year. Typical subject material may be selected from: design of advanced optical components, instruments and systems, detectors and instruments, the principles of laser radar (lidar), the interaction of laser radiation with materials, optical communication systems, advanced instrumentation for astronomy and space science.', 'GS', 'W'),
('dfe836d5-5e2a-4c81-b40a-a4ee85c34087', 'Selected Topics in Biological Physics', 'PHYS6180', 3.0, 'Discussion of one or more topics in biological physics. Specific topics will vary.', 'GS', 'F'),
('670f2fe3-82a3-47de-bd93-6ee32377ae43', 'Selected Topics in Physics', 'PHYS6213', 1.0, 'This course covers a selected topic in theoretical or experimental physics.', 'GS', 'F'),
('1b9cdf2b-889e-4e8c-b1c7-6fe49b2d919a', 'Selected Topics in Astronomy', 'PHYS6214', 1.0, 'This course covers a selected topic in theoretical or observational astronomy.', 'GS', 'W'),
('6a2c7117-162e-4b11-be4f-d72ac45917fb', 'Ph.D Research Evaluation', 'PHYS7001A', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'W'),
('45998686-e4f5-4200-9d4e-7ef43c8eff22', 'Ph.D. Research Evaluation', 'PHYS7001B', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'W'),
('f3bea284-6619-49cd-b07c-f6fceb66aeba', 'Ph.D. Research Evaluation', 'PHYS7001C', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'W'),
('bebc0aa7-d0e9-4d8d-b101-a0e587f691f5', 'Ph.D. Research Evaluation', 'PHYS7001D', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'W'),
('49a61068-1cd5-4732-993b-d3211b0686ec', 'Ph.D. Research Ecaluation', 'PHYS7001E', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'W'),
('65e4e82d-588d-4f88-b1f4-241799084ad8', 'Ph.D. Research Evaluation', 'PHYS7001F', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'W'),
('a1e836e3-635e-42f0-88cb-24063cfa28e5', 'Ph.D. Research Evaluation', 'PHYS7001G', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'W'),
('9a8297ef-1041-4987-84a4-614284c1a913', 'Public Management', 'PIA6000', 3.0, 'Examines the principles and processes of public management. It covers specific topics such as financial management, budget process, personnel management, political and administrative control. Analysis of proposals for public sector reform. Canadian and comparative experiences are studied.', 'GS', 'W'),
('862d6494-c327-461b-b362-1be27750fb80', 'Le processus d élaboration des politiques', 'PIA6001', 3.0, 'Examen des diverses étapes de lanalyse des politiques : définition, conception, mise en oeuvre et évaluation. Exploration de la gamme des théories et modèles de processus délaboration des politiques. Les expériences faites au Canada et dans dautres régimes comparables sont au programme.', 'GS', 'F'),
('bdc47e27-63e8-49ef-b255-808a784e7286', 'Economics and Public Policy', 'PIA6002', 3.0, 'Application of fundamental economic principles to the design and analysis of government programs and policies. The dilemma between equity/efficiency faced by governments in their interventions is addressed, as well as how economics devises optimal practical solutions to this problem.', 'GS', 'W'),
('9e4b4961-1098-42e8-9f6a-ef119ccaac1b', 'Research Methods', 'PIA6003', 3.0, 'Ce cours examine un vaste éventail de méthodes qualitatives et quantitatives afin dhabiliter les étudiants à utiliser, interpréter et analyser les données qualitatives et quantitatives ainsi quà évaluer les analyses réalisées par dautres.', 'GS', 'F'),
('28f94090-a3fe-49e8-bdf6-594b88122097', 'Capstone Seminar', 'PIA6100', 3.0, 'Students pursue individual projects, starting with policy briefs and culminating in major research papers or a policy analysis exercise. A major research paper is an independent examination of an issue in public affairs. A policy analysis exercise is developed in collaboration with a public sector organization, or client, exploring issues that confront the client and proposing recommendations. Drafts of texts are presented in the weekly seminars and the completed papers are presented in a year-end colloquium, attended by the full student body.', 'GS', 'W'),
('19aeee74-994f-4104-a798-5c0382f75066', 'Colloquium: Canada and its Place in the World', 'PIA6200', 1.5, 'Critical reflection on challenges facing Canada and the Canadian state, from both domestic and international perspectives. Sessions involve faculty members, visiting faculty, practitioners, members of School advisory committee, etc.', 'GS', 'F'),
('06f00ec9-a1d5-4a52-aca1-844e99f9a4d2', 'Colloquium: Canada and its Place in the World', 'PIA6200', 1.5, 'Critical reflection on challenges facing Canada and the Canadian state, from both domestic and international perspectives. Sessions involve faculty members, visiting faculty, practitioners, members of School advisory committee, etc.', 'GS', 'W'),
('84b77648-2c06-4ba4-b1b4-2cfe08fd1e5e', 'Migration mondiale et les lois et politiques du Canada.', 'PIA6303', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'F'),
('6e1933de-1e02-4bcd-8a46-07768c1a2b09', 'Le droit international et les organisations internationales', 'PIA6308', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'W'),
('9cd51348-fc02-4452-a577-ed23e2efbd66', 'The Environmental Crisis: International and Public Policy Implications', 'PIA6314', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'W'),
('221bacfb-1895-474a-8dbc-26b3b518ba1f', 'Théories, pratiques et histoire de la fonction diplomatique dans la modernité', 'PIA6337', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'F'),
('c976703d-2718-42c2-b8be-c8d62c8e0239', 'M.A. Colloquium', 'POLS6000A', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'Y'),
('4a7ce3ea-bf45-4019-9c3b-9a4246d520f1', 'Symposium In Political Theory', 'POLS6010', 6.0, '(Core Course) A comprehensive survey of representative works in traditional political thought from antiquity to the nineteenth century.', 'GS', 'Y'),
('29c75214-02c4-4bf7-95e1-fdf283a62967', 'French Post-Marxism and Radical Democratic Theory', 'POLS6021', 3.0, 'This advanced seminar will examine the emergence, in France, of a new theory of radical democracy born of the ruthless critique of totalitarian domination and of the discovery of a politics of emancipation in the wake of the events of May 1968. Inspired by Rosa Luxemburg''s alternative ''Socialism or Barbarism'', the anti-totalitarian left articulates a democratic project that remains critical of liberalism while rejecting vanguardism in the name of the political capacity of ''anybody and everybody'' (J. Rancière).', 'GS', 'F'),
('0e389ac1-6364-4a1d-8a6b-40a2af238197', 'African Socialism and Communism', 'POLS6036', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'F'),
('1b42d707-a6a1-4317-abcb-ec3512bed09e', 'Appropriating Marx''s Capital I', 'POLS6060', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'F'),
('3e9f3303-3d77-4085-b90a-7a872aa3486f', 'Global Politics and the ''Muslim World'' - Remapping the Figure of the Muslim in Contemporary Neoliberal Politics', 'POLS6073', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'F'),
('5bb2b4d4-52a0-497a-9142-a7e7d92b98a8', 'Thinking Power and Violence: from Nietzsche to Agamben', 'POLS6086', 3.0, 'About the meaning of power and violence as fundamental categories of modernity and human existence overall. The course is concerned with violence in many forms and manifestations, including: violence at the foundation of human community, conservative violence, ''divine violence,'' redemptive violence, self as violence against self and other, exclusionary violence, the violence of liberal freedom and the commodity, counter-hegemonic violence, the violence of the spectacle, the violence of outsiders and gender violence.', 'GS', 'F'),
('8849fcd7-2dd2-4620-b36d-0db14ca121d4', 'Politics of Aesthetics', 'POLS6087', 3.0, 'The Politics of Aesthetics develops an aesthetic framework from political and philosophical thinkers who have an aesthetic theory as part of their philosophy. These include Hegel, Kant, Heidegger, Vattimo, Badiou, Rancière and Zabala. The course is presented in blended(BLEN) format that includes in-class, on-line and print EE components: seminar presentation, seminar participation, interactive on-line discussion forum, one minute film, plus paper abstract and essay. The aim is for the student to be able to interact proficiently and seamlessly both online and in person to meet the requirements of a networked world.', 'GS', 'W'),
('6e5f27ee-c68a-4105-a72e-7d9bdcee04d6', 'Accelerating Technicity: ''Questions Concerning Technologies''', 'POLS6088', 3.0, 'Accelerating Technicity examines the concept of technology in select works of Heidegger, Marcuse, Deleuze, Simondon, Stiegler, Hayles, Virilio and Acclerationism. Using these theorists the course will grapple with Heidegger''s two conflicting tendencies in technology: the dominant tendency of instrumental technology (the danger inherent in technology) and second, the tendency toward poeisis (the revealing and saving potential inherent in technology). The course is presented in blended(BLEN)format that includes in-class, on-line and print EE components: seminar presentation, seminar participation, interactive on-line discussion forum, one minute film, plus paper abstract and essay. The aim is for the student to be able to interact proficiently and seamlessly both online and in person to meet the requirements of a networked world.', 'GS', 'F'),
('3e23ac5b-116a-46d1-9a6b-a6aabc0dd8f5', 'Canadian Government and Politics', 'POLS6110', 6.0, NULL, 'GS', 'Y'),
('6cc578d4-19f1-4e1f-ade3-9ac430b30f23', 'Indigenous Politics: Decolonization or "Development"?', 'POLS6145', 3.0, 'Explores indigenous development experiences in Canada and throughout the world, in comparative perspective. It draws on theories of development and underdevelopment and examines the sociology, politics and economics of development as well as environmental and cultural implications.', 'GS', 'F'),
('dccc3c61-4075-42f6-9b53-fc8d74a409ab', 'Democratic Administration', 'POLS6155', 3.0, '(Crosslisted to: GS/PPAL 6155 3.00, GS/LAW 6680 3.00)', 'GS', 'W'),
('d8b55d88-b305-4b38-87aa-4f6c96829de6', 'Adv. Study In International Relations', 'POLS6200', 6.0, NULL, 'GS', 'Y'),
('d7cda895-edb3-41ab-83ff-ec797da0f5e4', 'Hegemony, Imperialism and Globalization', 'POLS6205', 3.0, 'This course analyzes theories and concepts of power, supremacy, hegemony and imperialism in different world orders since antiquity. Analytical emphasis is placed on explaining the post-1945 period associated with American hegemony, Soviet Power and subsequent patterns of intensified globalization.', 'GS', 'W'),
('8b227bcc-284c-42b1-8220-acd7ba68b1f1', 'Contemporary Security Studies: Conflict, Intervention, and Peacebuilding', 'POLS6220', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'W'),
('0e642b31-752c-4a93-b724-a3e2669fee3f', 'Neoliberalism', 'POLS6250', 3.0, 'Examines the theories, practices, implicit rationalities, and tensions/contradictions of neoliberalism.', 'GS', 'W'),
('ace04c39-a64a-4fc7-9c78-7281c78c845a', 'Political Economy: Major Texts', 'POLS6271', 3.0, 'An in-depth introduction to major texts in the history of political economy, in this course we shall analyze texts by such thinkers as Adam Smith, Karl Marx, and J.M. Keynes with particular attention to issues having to do with methodology, the nature of the economic, and the relation of the economic to other areas of social life. Same as Social and Political Thought 6271 3.0', 'GS', 'W'),
('2a837a67-2c73-4648-b7fa-ce109deded44', 'Topics in Political Economy: Comparative and International I', 'POLS6280', 3.0, 'Examines historical structures of political economy at the levels of production, state and world order, with a special focus on structural change. A discussion and comparison of theoretical approaches.', 'GS', 'F'),
('5eda9931-6d5b-45d0-a7fb-7b224b6b431a', 'Illicit Economies and Global Politics', 'POLS6292', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'F'),
('b68aa912-0865-4202-a228-bfc4112fc568', 'Quantitative Analysis', 'POLS6340', 6.0, NULL, 'GS', 'Y'),
('ded14515-9d6c-407d-82fc-498532461771', 'Diasporas: Transnational Communities and Limits of Citizenship', 'POLS6525', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'W'),
('5bd32e9e-010b-421b-94d0-9a57428488ab', 'Convergences, Disparities, and Fault lines: Research in Latin American and Caribbean Studies', 'POLS6567', 3.0, '(Crosslisted to: GS/ANTH 6300 3.00, GS/ENVS 6300 3.00, GS/ANTH 5300 3.00, GS/HUMA 6168 3.00, GS/SOCI 6755 3.00)', 'GS', 'F'),
('1b0aae35-1478-4ec4-9f4d-afe9b72bf6c5', 'Advanced Topics in the Politics of the Global South: Developmental States in the Twenty-First Century', 'POLS6570', 3.0, 'This course seeks to theorize and empirically examine the dynamics of state-society-economy interactions in the Global South in the context of the experiences of neoliberal globalisation, new historical cases of state developmentalism and emerging alternative models of ''development''.', 'GS', 'F'),
('34de5e8f-0836-4f7c-b0c1-69c18de9a317', 'The Political Economy of the BRICS', 'POLS6625', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'W'),
('96ddd014-d6fb-4b69-bf88-ee9d52837791', 'Gender and International Human Rights: Law, Citizenships and Borders', 'POLS6705', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'F'),
('7a51b2f9-0038-46df-8715-21cef5cf17a3', 'Black Radical Thought', 'POLS6711', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'W'),
('4f7c9c8a-d60a-4dd8-8227-65b08b0823e3', 'Politics of AI, Ethics and Algorithmic Life', 'POLS6740', 3.0, 'This class takes an interdisciplinary approach to understand the political and ethical implications of the ubiquitous deployment of AI and Machine Learning technologies. We will engage in an intersectional and socio-technical perspective to critically assess the unevenly distributed impacts of automated decision making on communities. Some themes included are: bias and discrimination, surveillance, privacy, data feminism and data activism.', 'GS', 'F'),
('aab38583-82e1-43d6-9498-418455396c3f', 'Gender and the Construction of Global Markets', 'POLS6750', 3.0, '(Crosslisted to: GS/WMST 6113 3.00, GS/GFWS 6113 3.00)', 'GS', 'F'),
('52d32b0e-640e-4948-8653-098e2ae8ee28', 'The Political Economy of Work and Welfare', 'POLS6775', 3.0, '(Crosslisted to: GS/WMST 6207 3.00, GS/SOCI 6683 3.00, GS/SLST 6070 3.00, GS/GFWS 6207 3.00)', 'GS', 'W'),
('37acf57b-88e2-4da2-a267-31fd4129b66c', 'Reading Course', 'POLS6990', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'F'),
('58172782-465f-48f4-8501-c7bcce28f982', 'Reading Course', 'POLS6990', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'W'),
('b05833bd-8ccb-4791-909a-29f63dfa2014', 'Reading Course', 'POLS6990', 6.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'Y'),
('c415dde4-c4c4-4d43-bee8-e28846ababd9', 'Reading Course', 'POLS6990A', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'F'),
('0defab71-81d3-4e37-8def-8c7639878c06', 'Reading Course', 'POLS6990A', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'W'),
('338b43c1-6ba5-4aa6-9649-c188754a2024', 'Reading Course', 'POLS6990A', 6.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'Y'),
('8dff7e01-ef9d-4a32-a067-9f7f56bbdf5e', 'M.A. Major Research Paper', 'POLS6999', 6.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'F'),
('d53463f2-2c2b-4abc-bb73-08e1fe6c8301', 'Dissertation Proposal Workshop', 'POLS7000', 0.0, 'PhD III candidates are required to attend the PhD Dissertation Proposal Workshop. The proposal workshop consists of three three-hour sessions offered on a monthly basis during the Fall term of the academic year (with dates set for late September, October and November) plus two individual meeting with the Graduate Program Director to discuss their dissertation proposal, to set up a supervisory committee and to go over the draft thesis proposal. Students will receive a passing grade by attending the three sessions and a half-hour and one-hour meeting with the Graduate Program Director. The course involves 10.5 hours (nine seminar hours and 1.5 hours of individual meeting with the GPD) for the PhD student; the course involves 36 hours for the GPD (nine seminar hours plus 27 individual student hours', 'GS', 'F'),
('20faa2ec-3a19-4184-8898-f0869bdb780e', 'Public Management', 'PPAL6000', 3.0, 'An introduction to the major themes of Canadian public administration in the context of multicultural diversity and globalization. The course reviews the machinery of government, the development of public policy, the management of change, risk management, and business-government relations', 'GS', 'F'),
('a7bc7fe0-caa7-4dad-9b7a-d9c026188949', 'Leadership and Human Resources Management', 'PPAL6010', 3.0, 'With a particular emphasis on the public sector environment, this course examines the role of the leadership and the human resources management function in developing policies and practices which contribute to the achievement of public service objectives.', 'GS', 'Y'),
('e2bb1439-e63a-42d3-a279-238ac122ecb7', 'Canadian Constitutional Law', 'PPAL6100', 3.0, 'Provides students with the key concepts in public law - focusing on constitutional law - that they need to function effectively in the public sector at all levels. The impact of judicial review, the rule of law, multiculturalism and diversity are highlighted.', 'GS', 'Y'),
('e7c79f29-1892-4b4e-a859-2623224cc474', 'Administrative Law and Ethics: Principles and Practices in the Policy-Making Context', 'PPAL6120', 3.0, 'This course covers the basic principles and practices of administrative law. For instance, it addresses public sector ethics, and shows how the principles behind privacy and access to information legislation are derived from these ethical principles. Codes of ethics and ethics counsellors are examined.', 'GS', 'W'),
('5bb82343-a427-472a-8a2d-7b7c66fa94bc', 'Court Organization and Management', 'PPAL6140', 3.0, 'An overview of court administration in Canada, beginning with a study of judicial independence and the unique administrative problems this governing principle raises, the constitutional parameters of judicial administration, and court and tribunal administrative structures, and then moving to recent issues of court and tribunal administration such as case-flow management.', 'GS', 'W'),
('6a772284-4a00-450a-8634-031929306724', 'Democratic Administration', 'PPAL6155', 3.0, '(Crosslisted to: GS/POLS 6155 3.00, GS/LAW 6680 3.00)', 'GS', 'W'),
('6d8954ca-d190-4d75-98d8-ac202c1fa78d', 'Research Methods and Information Systems', 'PPAL6200', 3.0, 'Provides students with the basic knowledge necessary for the systematic collection of data in the public service, for understanding such data, and for using management information systems effectively, as well as an introduction to e-government.', 'GS', 'W'),
('6dc5d178-46d2-448f-ae3b-34e594c96491', 'Program Evaluation and Public Policy Analysis', 'PPAL6210', 3.0, 'Provides students with the analytical tools needed to evaluate a public sector program. This includes identification of goals and objectives, survey research methods, cost-benefit analysis, choosing appropriate indicators of success, and the role of program evaluation in the policy cycle.', 'GS', 'F'),
('6f7836cc-889c-495e-92bb-fd47425d4783', 'Topics in Public Policy', 'PPAL6230', 3.0, 'This course allows students to investigate areas of public policy of specific interest to them. Students meet in class and conduct an independent reading project to learn approaches to the analysis of public policy, undertake research, and present papers on their specific public policy interests/research.', 'GS', 'Y'),
('6395325f-269a-4faf-b45e-d3197eeb214a', 'Topics in Local and Regional Governance', 'PPAL6240', 3.0, 'This course equips students with a thorough understanding of local and regional government in Canada, along with the research skills to critically investigate and evaluate municipal government policy, process and management.', 'GS', 'Y'),
('81f410d0-dbf7-48de-aa5a-ccb2dfb73cab', 'Major Research Paper', 'PPAL6250', 3.0, 'An independent research project leading to a major research paper on a policy field of choice (e.g., e-government, health policy, environmental policy, justice policy, social welfare policy, transportation policy, foreign affairs policy, taxation policy). Students will have the opportunity to share their projects with other students in their cohort at a final research seminar.', 'GS', 'Y'),
('50776f63-e020-429d-82e7-06206962150b', 'Internship/Co-Op', 'PPAL6900', 3.0, 'This is the course equivalent for an internship/co-op placement consisting of work in a public or para-public institution related to public policy, administration and law; supervised by a committee of designated faculty members responsible for the academic evaluation. Placement supervisors provide written reports reflecting on the student''s work experience.', 'GS', 'F'),
('4be252b8-b92b-4036-b3e7-460651ba2e5c', 'Internship/Co-Op', 'PPAL6900', 3.0, 'This is the course equivalent for an internship/co-op placement consisting of work in a public or para-public institution related to public policy, administration and law; supervised by a committee of designated faculty members responsible for the academic evaluation. Placement supervisors provide written reports reflecting on the student''s work experience.', 'GS', 'W'),
('75a81bd7-0e9c-421e-9fa9-4f3fa96f177a', 'Internship/Co-Op', 'PPAL6900', 3.0, 'This is the course equivalent for an internship/co-op placement consisting of work in a public or para-public institution related to public policy, administration and law; supervised by a committee of designated faculty members responsible for the academic evaluation. Placement supervisors provide written reports reflecting on the student''s work experience.', 'GS', 'Y'),
('eb6604bf-6b87-459c-bc7d-cdec21d3a949', 'Independent Readings and Research', 'PPAL6990', 3.0, 'In exceptional cases, students in the Graduate Program in wishing to gain expertise in a particular area may take up to two three-credit independent reading and research courses with the approval of the graduate program director', 'GS', 'F'),
('b21cd16f-f5cb-4572-89ca-86f5add3cc80', 'Independent Readings and Research', 'PPAL6990', 3.0, 'In exceptional cases, students in the Graduate Program in wishing to gain expertise in a particular area may take up to two three-credit independent reading and research courses with the approval of the graduate program director', 'GS', 'W'),
('36ee75b2-97d8-49c4-9960-c81d92d3106a', 'Independent Readings and Research', 'PPAL6990', 3.0, 'In exceptional cases, students in the Graduate Program in wishing to gain expertise in a particular area may take up to two three-credit independent reading and research courses with the approval of the graduate program director', 'GS', 'Y'),
('e445197c-3c3d-45bb-857d-78735b820840', 'Seminar in Vision Research', 'PSYC6001', 0.0, 'Many humans and increasingly our technologies rely on vision to sense, represent, understand, and interact with the world. This course considers how we do so from a multi-disciplinary perspective. It is built around the highly-successful and long-standing CVR research seminar series, which serves as a framework for students to engage deeply with state-of-the-art cross-disciplinary vision research. This is a core course for the Collaborative Graduate Specialization in Vision Research.', 'GS', 'Y'),
('8d51d84c-06e4-4471-a247-47133c84fa54', 'Historical and Theoretical Foundations of Contemporary Psychology (A)', 'PSYC6020', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'F'),
('330311c7-7fbb-49b6-978d-9e5e7b368388', 'Historical and Theoretical Foundations of Contemporary Psychology (B)', 'PSYC6030', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'W'),
('9736ba5b-a6a7-419f-8793-64404c9f3d2d', 'Mental Health: History, Culture, and Critique', 'PSYC6063', 3.0, 'This course examines the experience of mental health from a historical and cultural perspective. It focuses on how understandings of mental health vary across time and place, looking at changes to the theories about its etiology alongside alterations to the arrangements of institutionalization, management, and care', 'GS', 'F'),
('75e058a7-7d9e-4a22-b52b-3f6acc5b5ddb', 'Mental Health: History, Culture, and Critique', 'PSYC6063', 3.0, 'This course examines the experience of mental health from a historical and cultural perspective. It focuses on how understandings of mental health vary across time and place, looking at changes to the theories about its etiology alongside alterations to the arrangements of institutionalization, management, and care', 'GS', 'W'),
('d9e7ce40-f4a3-496b-bba9-b58f98a37360', 'Critical Methods in and for Psychological Research', 'PSYC6066', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'W'),
('f953e66d-8590-4074-b353-1755c2bb6ead', 'Strategies for Data Management and Data Cleaning', 'PSYC6120', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'F'),
('61be0264-5ae2-4814-8cdc-79b092b7554d', 'General Linear Models I', 'PSYC6131', 3.0, 'The primary aim of this course is to provide the student with the basic tools for analyzing data from univariate designs with categorical predictors. The course material will focus on simple and complex Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) models.', 'GS', 'F'),
('76fa76fd-39f9-4ccc-acbb-da840a088f83', 'Univariate Analysis II: Regression', 'PSYC6132', 3.0, 'The primary aim of this course is to provide the student with the basic tools for analyzing data using regression models. The course will focus on the general linear model, encompassing multiple regression and ANOVA, thereby allowing any combination of continuous and categorical predictors. Time permitting, generalized linear models for categorical outcomes (e.g., logistic regression) may also be considered.', 'GS', 'W'),
('1d958099-99ca-47a5-9300-69266a136671', 'Experimental Research Methods in Social-Personality Psychology', 'PSYC6150B', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'W'),
('9adbf643-00f7-4b21-94d1-04ee6ac9aff9', 'Structural Equation Modeling', 'PSYC6176', 3.0, 'This course covers structural equalization modelling (SEM) concepts and methodology, including "causation" and correlation, path analysis, confirmatory factor analysis and latent variables. Students gain experience with computer programs (e.g., EQS) and practical applications. Previous knowledge of multivariate methods is desirable.', 'GS', 'W'),
('90d33c56-2fff-4ab3-952a-c589dc567c0f', 'Psychometric Methods', 'PSYC6180', 3.0, 'This course will expand on the psychological measurement topics covered in undergraduate psychological testing and measurement courses. Prerequisite: GS/PSYC 6130 3.00 or an equivalent statistics course covering ANOVA and regression.', 'GS', 'F'),
('fe62b21b-a11c-4b11-95e3-f72c4279905f', 'Neuroimaging of Cognition - fMRI Methods', 'PSYC6226', 3.0, 'This course offers fundamental knowledge on neuroimaging of cognition using fMRI, including practical aspects of experimental design and analytical approaches. The course provides the necessary theoretical perspectives of fMRI experiments and provides extensive "hands-on" experience in fMRI analysis.', 'GS', 'F'),
('611daa1a-be17-424b-951c-21d1da8f7538', 'Brain Mechanisms of Movement in Health and Disease', 'PSYC6235', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'W'),
('b5238a02-44d4-42a2-b0fe-6ba44f535cb9', 'Technology''s role in healthy aging', 'PSYC6240', 3.0, 'Explore the relationship between technology, aging, and psychology, emphasizing the relevance of how different technology can address older adults (OA) health needs by fostering inclusion and promoting access for OA. Examine factors that can impact technology use among OA, including cognitive aging, social isolation/loneliness, and the challenges and opportunities associated with emerging technologies'' impact on quality of life.', 'GS', 'F'),
('4d7e3443-b314-4787-8695-22b389ac5826', 'Fundamentals of Neuroscience II: Circuits, Systems and Behaviour', 'PSYC6253', 3.0, 'This course focuses on a systems approach to specialized circuits within the central nervous system that determine sensory, motor and cognitive systems. Permission of the Instructors is required to enrol in the course.', 'GS', 'W'),
('381af195-2c3f-47f1-b8fb-1d3539ed2bce', 'Fundamentals of Neuroscience I: Structures, Neurons and Synapses', 'PSYC6257', 3.0, 'This course will focus on molecular and cellular mechanisms underlying the structure and function of the nervous system, functional neuroanatomy, and the neurophysiology of movement. Prerequisites: undergraduate course in neuroscience or equivalent or by permission of Instructor', 'GS', 'F'),
('f8f71002-66a9-4dc0-84e3-e11f188f35d9', 'Computer Programming for Experimental Psychology', 'PSYC6273', 3.0, 'This graduate course covers computer programming methods that are useful for running experiments and analyzing data in experimental psychology. Students will learn a general-purpose programming language such as MATLAB or Python. Topics include basic programming methods and data structures, data files, curve fitting, device calibration, data visualization, statistical tests, model simulations, and interfacing to external devices.', 'GS', 'F'),
('7008bb3f-aaf0-41de-9698-279739817066', 'Principles of Human Perception and Performance in Human-Computer Interactions/Same as Computer Science 6326 3.0', 'PSYC6315', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'F'),
('7a634696-0c6c-400c-bf67-42b483a8fed6', 'Cognitive Neurorehabilitation: Basic Science to Clinical Application', 'PSYC6330', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'F'),
('7faa83c9-93e4-488e-b50a-592260079e01', 'Contemporary Issues in Personality and Social Psychology', 'PSYC6400', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'W'),
('c194ee50-3eee-49e9-a55e-f7470b5c975d', 'Foundations of Clinical Psychology: Biopsychosocial formulations', 'PSYC6421', 3.0, 'This course provides a biopsychosocial perspective on severe forms of mental illness and cognitive impairment including schizophrenia, bipolar mood disorder, dementia, and other disturbances of brain and behaviour. Consideration will be given to biological and environmental contributions to etiology, prevention and treatment and to the sociocultural and cross-cultural contexts of these disorders. The role of psychologists as scientist-clinicians in advancing understanding and intervention options for serious mental illness is highlighted.', 'GS', 'W'),
('a7027f4b-2fe8-4707-9ece-fd3f3bd1b06d', 'Foundations of Clinical Psychology: Psychopathology and Personality', 'PSYC6422', 3.0, 'This course is an introduction to a knowledge base underlying the theory and practice of clinical psychology, with an emphasis on theories of the self (e.g., cognitive, psychodynamic, developmental) and how personality contributes to psychopathology. It comprises an integrative and critical review of theory and research on mental disorders, including depressive, anxiety, obsessive-compulsive, substance, trauma and stress-related, and eating disorders.', 'GS', 'F'),
('b7f52a7b-f14f-44e4-90b6-e11b24c24c16', 'Clinical Practicum I', 'PSYC6430P', 6.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'Y'),
('cd275b05-37cd-4010-8b51-e585cd26ec45', 'Cognitive Assessment', 'PSYC6431', 3.0, 'This course covers the theoretical foundations of cognitive assessment and related psychometric and scale development concepts. There is a practical component that centres on test administration, scoring, interpretation, and clinical report writing. Clinical and professional issues surrounding cognitive assessment will be discussed throughout the course.', 'GS', 'F'),
('27ccdf07-e6ee-4529-b137-7943a858eb35', 'Personality Assessment', 'PSYC6432', 3.0, 'This course covers psychometric and theoretical foundations and applied aspects of personality assessment. Tests commonly used in clinical practice are examined at a theoretical and practical level with emphasis on procedures of test score interpretation, profile analysis, and report writing. Clinical and professional issues surrounding personality assessment are discussed throughout the course.', 'GS', 'W'),
('8ebfa3da-8672-45f1-b5ff-471f75a32860', 'Evidence Based Principles of Psychotherapy', 'PSYC6436', 3.0, 'This course is informed by a common factors framework and provides students with grounding in fundamental principles of psychotherapy. These include a framework for viewing all forms of psychotherapy, and consideration of the therapeutic alliance and alliance ruptures, empathy, awareness & experiencing, emotion and emotion regulation, and client & therapist factors known to influence psychotherapy process and outcomes. In addition, the course will cover case formulation, issues regarding empirically supported treatments, and the three major specific models of psychotherapy: psychodynamic, cognitive-behavioral, and person-centred or person-experiential.', 'GS', 'F'),
('22382d9a-3209-4d31-88b0-6d94c2336d5d', 'Approaches to Psychotherapy: Advanced Study', 'PSYC6437', 3.0, 'Provides students with advanced and intensive study of major models of psychotherapy, from traditional to contemporary. Models include psychodynamic, cognitive-behavioral, and person-centred or person-experiential, but also contemporary models including emotion-focused therapy, post-modern approaches and narrative processes, motivational interviewing, mindfulness & acceptance-based approaches. In addition, other modalities of therapy are considered including group therapy, systems approaches, and couples therapy. While each model is considered individually, psychotherapy integration will also be discussed and considered throughout the course.', 'GS', 'W'),
('adc4f64e-7e57-4722-a059-16b718059a30', 'Clinical Practicum II', 'PSYC6440P', 6.0, 'In this course, students are given practical training in psychodiagnosis and in advanced approaches to psychological intervention. The training is mediated through demonstration, role playing, and supervised management of a small number of clinical cases. Prerequisites: Psychology 6420.06, Psychology 6430.06, Psychology 6435.06 and Psychology 6430P.06.', 'GS', 'Y'),
('f6907656-44b7-4f28-8e93-4a2b96042fec', 'Psychodiagnostics', 'PSYC6441P', 6.0, 'This course covers the theoretical foundations, psychometric knowledge, and practical skills required to conduct a comprehensive psychological assessment. Students learn about the psychometric basis for core foundational psychological tests, practice test administration, and learn to interpret and integrate test scores with clinical history and observations. Students conduct a real-life assessment together with classmates.', 'GS', 'Y'),
('9fb956cb-1e53-4fc6-8cbe-88b823581d76', '"Advanced Psychological Intervention" as a practicum', 'PSYC6445P', 6.0, 'Provides advanced training in theory of intervention. Specific methods of active psychotherapeutic intervention and research evidence on their impact will be covered. This course includes more broad ranging rehabilitation and systems approaches to intervention. Prerequisites: GS/PSYC 6420 6.00, GS/PSYC 6430P 6.00, GS/PSYC 6435 6.00.', 'GS', 'Y'),
('6a4f204d-6d0e-4b03-98cc-b378e5f91408', 'Graduate Diploma in Health Psychology - Seminar 1', 'PSYC6456', 1.5, 'Provides the basics of a broad and systemic training in health psychology. The course is not only intended to be educational but also a supportive environment for students as they progress through their graduate training.', 'GS', 'Y'),
('3d763595-af00-4506-9b08-c57bb1cf1f98', 'Graduate Diploma in Health Psychology - Seminar 2', 'PSYC6457', 1.5, 'Provides the basics of a broad and systemic training in health psychology. The course is not only intended to be educational but also a supportive environment for students as they progress through their graduate training.', 'GS', 'Y'),
('e5015c92-8fda-4ee8-9c0f-128c2eea0681', 'Clinical Practicum III (Optional)', 'PSYC6460P', 6.0, 'This practicum provides extra clinical training that the student and the Director of Clinical Training deem necessary for the student to meet training requirements, such as assuring that the student has sufficient training in both assessment and intervention skills. This practicum may be taken only after the Ph.D. III academic year and provided that the student has completed all course work, and either the minor area paper or an approved Ph.D. dissertation proposal.', 'GS', 'Y'),
('e279bab0-b373-46a7-9510-aa81c329ea0d', 'Clinical Practicum III (Optional)', 'PSYC6460P', 3.0, 'This practicum provides extra clinical training that the student and the Director of Clinical Training deem necessary for the student to meet training requirements, such as assuring that the student has sufficient training in both assessment and intervention skills. This practicum may be taken only after the Ph.D. III academic year and provided that the student has completed all course work, and either the minor area paper or an approved Ph.D. dissertation proposal.', 'GS', 'W'),
('ba7ea9a4-08b1-4cce-8266-bb4e1446e96a', 'Clinical Practicum III (Optional)', 'PSYC6460P', 3.0, 'This practicum provides extra clinical training that the student and the Director of Clinical Training deem necessary for the student to meet training requirements, such as assuring that the student has sufficient training in both assessment and intervention skills. This practicum may be taken only after the Ph.D. III academic year and provided that the student has completed all course work, and either the minor area paper or an approved Ph.D. dissertation proposal.', 'GS', 'F'),
('fc532d58-b385-47bd-837b-6ca76dcd932e', 'Stress, Coping and Health', 'PSYC6465', 3.0, 'This course reviews theory, methods, and findings on stress and coping in relation to physical and mental health. Topics include measurement and conceptualization of stress, coping, and outcome plus the study of their inter-relations."', 'GS', 'W'),
('8c74c69f-96e2-4480-aead-4561dca5d2fc', 'Introduction to Qualitative Research', 'PSYC6474', 3.0, 'An introduction to qualitative research methods. The main methods of qualitative research are surveyed, followed by concentration on the particular method(s) with which the Instructor has expertise. In this concentration, the given methods procedures are specified and exercises are provided to give the student first-hand experience at using them. The overall goal of the course is to provide a sufficient grounding in qualitative research methodology to enable students to apply it competently.', 'GS', 'F'),
('18cb68db-3e60-4701-bcd3-6a639c76c2a2', 'Introduction to Qualitative Research', 'PSYC6474', 3.0, 'An introduction to qualitative research methods. The main methods of qualitative research are surveyed, followed by concentration on the particular method(s) with which the Instructor has expertise. In this concentration, the given methods procedures are specified and exercises are provided to give the student first-hand experience at using them. The overall goal of the course is to provide a sufficient grounding in qualitative research methodology to enable students to apply it competently.', 'GS', 'W'),
('6562c4d7-4da5-4423-bfe7-6aa42b7c2e01', 'Personality', 'PSYC6510', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'W'),
('a7d920d5-5564-402c-9a3b-5a68b5ff99df', 'Programme Evaluation', 'PSYC6520B', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'F'),
('e66cc7a1-b03f-4592-9e8e-bb8c67e2605b', 'Social and Emotional Bases of Development', 'PSYC6610', 3.0, 'An advanced general course in development covering: perceptual learning and/or perceptual motor skills; learning; cognitive processes and intelligence; social and personality development, language development, and any other area of development of current interest to the developmental area.', 'GS', 'F'),
('5bdda674-bd49-4683-aaa2-f133b24ccfe9', 'Developing the Visual Brain', 'PSYC6635', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'W'),
('65e4a661-4a6d-49bd-9b3d-286d00a944e4', 'Readings', 'PSYC6710', 6.0, 'A reading course for a student or group of students with a faculty member in any area not covered by the psychology courses offered in a particular year. Note: Enrolments in this course are limited and are authorized for individual students only on the specific recommendation of the Graduate Programme Director. See Program Director for specific procedures involved.', 'GS', 'Y'),
('607fdf7b-fc7c-4040-b07a-2620719742a5', 'Readings', 'PSYC6710', 3.0, 'A reading course for a student or group of students with a faculty member in any area not covered by the psychology courses offered in a particular year. Note: Enrolments in this course are limited and are authorized for individual students only on the specific recommendation of the Graduate Programme Director. See Program Director for specific procedures involved.', 'GS', 'F'),
('6f8edb88-814e-42d3-8345-bdb732dfdf15', 'Readings', 'PSYC6710', 3.0, 'A reading course for a student or group of students with a faculty member in any area not covered by the psychology courses offered in a particular year. Note: Enrolments in this course are limited and are authorized for individual students only on the specific recommendation of the Graduate Programme Director. See Program Director for specific procedures involved.', 'GS', 'W'),
('d5724c01-0224-4ba6-b7d3-ddcc7ae7ff1c', 'Rhythms of the Brain', 'PSYC6805', 3.0, 'The course explores the form and function of neuronal oscillations. Specific topics addressed include classes of inhibitory circuits, sleep rhythms, hippocampal rhythms, neocortical oscillations and their behavioural correlates. Prerequisite: Psychology 6253/Integrated with the undergraduate course Health Psychology 4380 3.0/Psychology 6235 3.0, or permission from course instructor', 'GS', 'W'),
('dab69e7d-54cc-449b-8b27-d6f102dbbc3e', 'Applied Practicum II', 'PSYC6810B', 3.0, 'Supervised experience in the application of the principles of psychology to the amelioration of applied problems.', 'GS', 'F'),
('5ed7ae7c-9d6b-4080-8a59-5d94aaaa5fc0', 'Applied Practicum II', 'PSYC6810B', 3.0, 'Supervised experience in the application of the principles of psychology to the amelioration of applied problems.', 'GS', 'W'),
('65951282-6741-4bd3-ba7d-dc86254b936c', 'Applied Practicum I', 'PSYC6810A', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'F'),
('c5732d42-cbdf-43b7-90c5-aa77e9103722', 'Applied Practicum I', 'PSYC6810A', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'W'),
('86d674da-6d01-4b85-a6ca-ca0a2e91a0d8', 'Applied Practicum I', 'PSYC6810A', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'Y'),
('54ad23e8-9ebe-442c-a2c9-6b0b77af295b', 'Applied Practicum III', 'PSYC6810C', 6.0, 'Supervised experience in the application of the principles of psychology to the amelioration of applied problems.', 'GS', 'Y'),
('7e13f232-6a9f-4d5e-b058-8cd5852bebb3', 'Applied Practicum II', 'PSYC6810B', 6.0, 'Supervised experience in the application of the principles of psychology to the amelioration of applied problems.', 'GS', 'Y'),
('20d41dc2-7814-45a2-bf32-e0255c462b42', 'Research Practicum I', 'PSYC6820A', 3.0, 'Supervised experience in the design, execution, analysis and communication of psychological research.', 'GS', 'F'),
