('43f57107-d918-4f78-87df-b81d46ccc0b1', 'Introduction to Computational Problem Solving', 'DIGT1101', 4.0, 'DIGT1101 Introduces students to basic computational problem solving through basic procedural programming. Topics include elementary computing problems and corresponding iterative solutions using constructs such as branching, loops and nested loops, input/output, as well as simple data structures such as primitive data types, strings, and arrays. The laboratory component of the course focuses on problem solving using a high-level programming language. Related mathematical topics such as propositional logic, sets, and functions are introduced as needed. Course credit exclusions: LE/CSSD 1101 3.00, LE/CSSD 1102 3.00, LE/EECS 1011 3.00, LE/EECS 1012 3.00, LE/EECS 1015 3.00, LE/EECS 1710 3.00.', 'LE', 'F'),
('f9182c2a-271b-4b4b-ac87-328e5fee692e', 'Teamwork and Communication in Software Development', 'DIGT1161', 2.0, 'This course is focused on teamwork and communication in developing software projects. Topics include communication within centralized, decentralized, and global software teams, collaborative software development, version control systems, issue repositories, open-sourced, crowdsourced, and outsourced software, knowledge sharing and Q/A platforms, ethics, individual and team productivity, diversity, and inclusion within teams. Course credit exclusions: LE/CSSD 1161 2.00.', 'LE', 'Y'),
('7ffa1dcf-d87a-4373-b80b-a9b7162ab33b', 'Being a Digital Citizen', 'DIGT1172', 3.0, 'In this course, students are critically introduced to the digital world they inhabit. Students identify, discuss, and apply concepts of digital ethics to questions and the search for answers in the digital world, with a focus on communicating the development and effects these tools and technologies have on our lives. Driving this course is the question: ''What do we need to know and do in relation to the past and present of digital technologies to be active, ethical, and literate digital citizens?''', 'LE', 'F'),
('f9524a4f-cf9b-405b-b3f2-5d6798aa9e93', 'Object-Oriented Problem Solving', 'DIGT1201', 8.0, 'DIGT1201 introduces students to the fundamentals of object-oriented programming and problem-solving skills. Students will gain knowledge and computational skills related to designing object-oriented solutions, using APIs (Application Programming Interfaces), inheritance, composition, event-driven programming, and recursion. They will also learn about professional software development processes such as test-driven development, debugging, software versioning, documentation, and agile methodologies. Related mathematical topics such as proof techniques, loop invariants, and asymptotic analysis are introduced as needed. Prerequisites: LE/DIGT 1101 4.00 or LE/CSSD 1101 3.00 or LE/CSSD 1102 3.00. Course credit exclusions: LE/CSSD 1201 4.00, LE/CSSD 2101 4.00, LE/EECS 1021 3.00, LE/EECS 1022 3.00, LE/EECS 2030 3.00.', 'LE', 'WS'),
('3a9631ba-86d3-4e4e-aeda-b64dd9696cf1', 'Mobilizing Digital Citizenship', 'DIGT1272', 3.0, 'In this course, students build on the foundation laid by the prerequisite ''Being a Digital Citizen'' to collaboratively develop and share a digital prototype that takes into account questions of ethics and equity for the present and the future. Driving this course is the question: ''How do we understand and choose the tools and technologies to communicate/advocate for ideas/organizations/resources online?''. Prerequisite: LE/DIGT 1172 3.00.', 'LE', 'W'),
('aedf3c5e-7192-44e0-a5ac-6dfafb198739', 'Data Structures, Algorithms & Analysis', 'DIGT2102', 8.0, 'DIGT 2102 introduces fundamental data structures and algorithms as well as asymptotic algorithm analysis. Topics include arrays, linked lists, hash tables, queues, search trees, sorting algorithms, graph data structures, elementary graph algorithms (traversal, shortest path, topological sort), and algorithm design techniques such as greedy algorithms, divide and conquer, dynamic programming, and stochastic algorithms. The course spans three terms (F, W, S). The course starts with simple data structures and introduces increasingly complex data structures while gradually delving into algorithmic design. The course is organized into three distinct terms, each adding more complexity both on data structures and algorithms. Throughout each term, students will design and implement one or two software applications that require the use of fundamental data structures and search algorithms. Students will develop their communication skills and enhance their e-portfolio. Prerequisites: LE/DIGT 1201 8.00. Course credit exclusions: LE/CSSD 2102 4.00, LE/CSSD 2202 4.00, LE/EECS 2101 3.00, LE/EECS 3101 3.00.', 'LE', 'FS'),
('33aeb7c9-1545-4c1e-8313-cd0c384b4849', 'Introduction to Computer Organization & Systems', 'DIGT2103', 4.0, 'DIGT 2103 introduces the basics of computer organization and systems. Topics include access to memory through implicit and explicit memory references, data segment, program, heap, and stack; fetch-execute cycle; translating data access, logic and code blocks written in highlevel languages and translated into Assembly language; memory management bugs, dangling pointers, memory leaks, and best practices in memory management; implementation of program features such as functions and polymorphism in a hardware model; performance in CPUs, pipelining, and memory caching. Prerequisites: LE/DIGT 1201 8.00. Course credit exclusions: LE/CSSD 2103 0.50, LE/CSSD 2104 2.00, LE/CSSD 2105 1.00, LE/CSSD 2106 0.50, LE/EECS 2021 4.00, LE/EECS 2031 3.00.', 'LE', 'Y'),
('cac68f08-3d8a-4c64-9842-30c91a25d36d', 'Practice of Software Development', 'DIGT2107', 3.0, 'In this course, students develop a software system such as web application or desktop application, over a period of twelve months. The developed software may be part of their on-the-job workflow, or it can be for a community partner. This project may be a component of a large-scale system which the student is contributing to at their workplace. Elements include organizing, writing, testing and debugging software in an IDE; designing software using common OO design patterns (e.g., Singleton, Observer); describing and documenting requirements; creating a system description at an appropriate level of abstraction; deriving and implementing unit and system level tests. Prerequisites: LE/DIGT1201 8.00. Co-requisites: LE/DIGT 2102 8.00. Course credit exclusions: LE/EECS 2311 3.00.', 'LE', 'FS'),
('cb9c681a-fb6e-42f2-93df-d93234d62763', 'Introduction to Data Science', 'DIGT2201', 3.0, 'DIGT2201 provides students with basic knowledge and skills for computation, analysis, and visualization of data using software tools. It overviews and introduces basic mathematical foundations, libraries, basic machine learning algorithms for supervised, unsupervised and reinforcement learning, as well as different areas that require data science. It guides students through the design process of data science projects using these elements as building blocks. It briefly introduces ethical issues in data science. Prerequisites: SC/MATH 1516 3.00, LE/DIGT 1201 8.00. Course credit exclusions: LE/CSSD 2231 3.00.', 'LE', 'W'),
('04b65e3e-0286-449c-a447-5c67fc9ed3e0', 'Computer Networks', 'DIGT2206', 3.0, 'This course allows students to understand how computer networks are put together, how they work, how applications use them, and what the underlying fundamental principles are that allow developers to build and design applications using computer networks. The course focuses on the design of programs that use computer networks; the terminology associated with networking; the key paradigms and strategies used in developing applications that use computer networks; the basic concepts of how the Internet is put together and operates; and the basic protocols that are used in the TCP/IP architecture. The material will be framed by looking at the key strategies and models for addressing scalability and reliability; layers and abstractions; routing, naming, and addressing; isolation, data loss, and performance; and privacy and security. Prerequisites: LE/DIGT 1302 3.00. Course credit exclusions: LE/EECS 3214 3.00, LE/CSSD 3106 2.00.', 'LE', 'W'),
('c5048bc7-baab-4ac2-91a8-acd1cc25a66d', 'Software Development Lifecycle', 'DIGT3101', 3.0, 'This course introduces the Software Development Lifecycle (SDLC) which covers a comprehensive study of the processes, methodologies, and best practices employed in developing high-quality software. Some of the methodologies are, among others, Agile, Waterfall, and Iterative. The course equips students with the knowledge and skills necessary to navigate the entire software development journey, from inception, specification, coding, testing, to deployment and maintenance. By exploring different SDLC methodologies and understanding each phase''s significance, students will be well-prepared to contribute effectively to real-world software projects. Prerequisites: LE/DIGT 2107 3.00', 'LE', 'FS'),
('972c23a2-f5f9-4663-b210-d37e93371641', 'Data Management Systems', 'DIGT3107', 3.0, 'DIGT 3107 convers concepts, approaches, and techniques in data management systems. Topics include logical models of relational databases, relational database design, query languages, crash recovery, and concurrency control. The purpose of this course is to introduce the fundamental concepts of database management, including aspects of data models, database languages, and database design. At the end of this course, a student will be able to understand and apply the fundamental concepts required for the design and administration of data management systems. Pre-requisites: LE/DIGT 2102 8.00. Course credit exclusions: LE/CSSD 3201 3.00.', 'LE', 'Y'),
('26287753-c84d-4825-b9f7-6537e2a82f1e', 'Network Security', 'DIGT3121', 3.0, 'This course extends the discussion of network security covered by DIGT 2206, focusing on how network protocols, applications and platforms implement confidentiality, authentication, integrity, privacy, access and availability. Students are presented with both theoretical discussions of network security fundamental principles, but also with practical applications of these principles in real-life protocols and applications. Strategies used to provide security in network environments are evaluated in the context of scalability and reliability, layers and abstractions, isolation and performance. Pre-requisites: LE/DIGT 2206 3.00, LE/DIGT 2302 3.00. Course credit exclusions: LE/CSSD 3221 3.00.', 'LE', 'WS'),
('829ff512-146a-4526-931f-377f83a723bd', 'Introduction to Machine Learning I', 'DIGT3131', 3.0, 'DIGT3131 is the first course of the Introduction to Machine Learning series (DIGT 3131 and DIGT 3231). DIGT3131 and DIGT 3231 together present the fundamentals of machine learning. DIGT3131 covers basic mathematical concepts, introduces machine learning principles and supervised learning with a focus on regression. Students will learn to implement and evaluate regression algorithms using Python and libraries like Scikit-learn, applying these algorithms to real-world datasets. Emphasis will also be placed on model evaluation and ethical considerations in machine learning, including fairness and transparency. This course aims to prepare students for topics in machine learning in DIGT3231, and to foster a responsible and ethical approach to the development and deployment of machine learning models. Prerequisites: LE/DIGT 2201 3.00. Credit credit exclusions: LE/EECS 3404 3.00, LE/EECS 3405 3.00, LE/CSSD 3131 3.00, LE/CSSD 3231 3.00.', 'LE', 'Y'),
('3fba94f9-d61d-4499-be92-7f58b2338c7b', 'Software I: Software Design and Architecture', 'DIGT3141', 3.0, 'DIGT 3141 covers fundamental software engineering principles and development of skills for the design and implementation of software systems. Topics include basic principles of software development processes with emphasis on agile methods and test-driven development, and UML-based software design specification. The course introduces foundational software engineering principles related to the design of software systems, in-depth analysis of software systems architectural styles, design patterns, specification and design of APIs, antipatterns and antipattern detection strategies, refactoring, principles of distributed software architectures, metrics and standards for assessing software design quality assurance practices and communicating design decisions through software design models. Prerequisites: LE/DIGT 2107 3.00. Course credit exclusions: LE/EECS 3311 3.00, LE/CSSD 2203 4.00.', 'LE', 'Y'),
('a55652dd-c62d-4a6e-9392-2b5d14e7de29', 'Digital Technologies Project', 'DIGT3171', 9.0, 'DIGT 3171 is a comprehensive, year-long capstone course designed for third-year students in the Digital Technologies Program. Spanning the fall, winter, and summer terms, this 9-credit course offers students a unique opportunity to apply their theoretical knowledge to a full-sized project, potentially integrated with their current workplace. Through designing, implementing, and managing a significant digital technology project, students will enhance their professional skills, including problem-solving, communication, and project management, while gaining valuable insights into industry trends. This course culminates in a final presentation and comprehensive documentation, equipping students with a portfolio piece that showcases their expertise and readiness for a professional career in digital technologies. Pre-requisites : LE/DIGT 2107 3.00', 'LE', 'FS'),
('c569c79c-3ff0-46f8-b8ed-6c945d4f06d5', 'Distributed Systems', 'DIGT3203', 3.0, 'DIGT 3203 aims to present the foundations of distributed systems. Its content is centered around four main areas: distributed systems architecture, inter-process communication and coordination, computer architecture, and the fundamentals of network communication for distributed computing. Students will learn to design and develop components that can communicate and coordinate with each other through a network, access data resources securely, and operate efficiently and reliably. Additionally, the course will explore how concepts previously encountered only in local contexts, such as data and operating systems, must be adapted for effective use in distributed environments. Throughout the course, students will gain practical experience by developing multi-threaded applications with concurrent data access in a distributed setting, utilizing services and resource virtualization. Pre-requisites: LE/DIGT 2206 3.00. Course credit exclusions: LE/CSSD 3211 3.0, LE/EECS 4222 3.00.', 'LE', 'WS'),
('22848c99-681d-4866-9c14-fd6504b26421', 'Security and Privacy: Awareness and Governance', 'DIGT3206', 3.0, 'DIGT 3206 aims to raise awareness for the digital citizen and the developer about digital technology security and privacy concerns. It introduces fundamental principles and frameworks for cyber risk assessment and management, and their role in risk governance, from individual assets to whole-system goals and objectives. It presents the landscape of privacy threats, law, and engineering mechanisms, with a special focus on threats arising due to artificial intelligence and machine learning. It discusses the general legal and ethical frameworks on security, safety and privacy. Finally, it presents security and threat-aware design frameworks and their applicability in different settings. Pre-requisites: LE/DIGT 2302 3.00.', 'LE', 'WS'),
('705a8c1d-57ee-4e59-94d3-25a066cbf897', 'Security by Design', 'DIGT3221', 3.0, 'DIGT 3221 focuses on security by design. The course integrates software design principles with security concerns. The course''s objective is to let the student understand the importance of guaranteeing the security of digital systems as early as a project''s design phase. Students will review software design principles and design patterns and how these can be applied to ensure security. They will also recognize security antipatterns and learn how to resolve them by refactoring the design of the system. They will also learn how to do threat modeling in parallel with designing the system. Pre-requisites: LE/DIGT 2302 3.00', 'LE', 'Y'),
('62611bfa-223d-41d9-ac77-786e274ff0fb', 'Introduction to Machine Learning II', 'DIGT3231', 3.0, 'DIGT3231 is the second course of the Introduction to Machine Learning series (DIGT 3131 and DIGT 3231). DIGT3131 and DIGT 3231 together present an introduction to machine learning. DIGT 3231 covers more advanced mathematical concepts, and techniques such as neural networks, unsupervised learning, model evaluation, and feature selection. Students will learn to implement and evaluate various machine learning algorithms using Python and libraries like Scikit-learn, applying these algorithms to real-world datasets. By the end of the course, students will be equipped to tackle practical problems in diverse fields and have a solid basis for further study and careers in data science and artificial intelligence. Pre-requisites: LE/DIGT 2201 3.00. Co-requisites: LE/DIGT 3131 3.00. Course credit exclusions: LE/EECS 3404 3.00, LE/EECS 3405 3.00, LE/CSSD 3131 3.00, LE/CSSD 3231 3.00.', 'LE', 'WS'),
('04224f91-8adc-4a32-9f41-c755a1b8a0a3', 'Software II: Software Testing and Quality Assurance', 'DIGT3241', 3.0, 'DIGT 3241 aims to introduce key techniques and tools for testing software systems at unit level, subsystem level, and application level as well as techniques to assess and quantify the quality of a software component or a software application as a whole. This course covers topics in black box unit testing, white box unit testing, grey box testing, elements of data flow analysis, object-oriented systems testing, model-driven testing, bottom-up, top-down and meet-in-the-middle integration testing, functional testing and, acceptance testing. In addition to the above, the course will cover topics related to different perspectives of load and penetration testing software quality, software metrics, code walkthroughs, and software reliability growth models. Students will learn to design test cases at all phases of the testing life cycle (unit, integration, functional, acceptance), use standard frameworks to test a software system, and evaluate the quality and reliability of a software system using code analysis and reliability growth models. Throughout the term, students will gain practical experience by designing and applying test plans and test cases on medium size systems, evaluate the testing effort, and quantify the impact testing and code walkthroughs have on increasing system quality and reliability. Pre-requisites: LE/DIGT 2107 3.00. Co-requisites: LE/DIGT 3141 3.00. Course credit exclusions: LE/CSSD 3203 3.00, LE/EECS 4313 3.00.', 'LE', 'WS'),
