('ffa70dca-16ae-4f11-b589-9c757d9f3703', 'Tech Knowledge Training: Video and Sound Editing with Davinci Resolve', 'PANF1100A', 1.0, 'In this micro-credit course, students will learn techniques for creative media production using specific software. These short intensive courses offer technical training in how to navigate application interfaces, create media, and incorporate into a wider production ecosystem. Open to non-majors.', 'FA', 'F'),
('24d52f17-e294-4928-9c2a-5c5a5ef3eff8', 'Tech Knowledge Training: 3D with Blender', 'PANF1100B', 1.0, 'In this micro-credit course, students will learn techniques for creative media production using specific software. These short intensive courses offer technical training in how to navigate application interfaces, create media, and incorporate into a wider production ecosystem. Open to non-majors.', 'FA', 'F'),
('fdb03292-f810-4db7-aab7-b0a032826073', 'Tech Knowledge Training: Web Coding HTML/CSS/JS', 'PANF1100C', 1.0, 'In this micro-credit course, students will learn techniques for creative media production using specific software. These short intensive courses offer technical training in how to navigate application interfaces, create media, and incorporate into a wider production ecosystem. Open to non-majors.', 'FA', 'W'),
('b7aa410e-5cc3-47b5-9644-6390ca04e77d', 'Multimedia Asset Creation I', 'PANF1200', 3.0, 'Explores the techniques of non-linear multimedia asset creation as used in sound, video, and image editing software tools such as video editors, visual effects (VFX) software, digital audio workstations (DAW), digital audio editors, and vector and raster image editors. Topics of the course techniques are essential for asset creation in computational arts including the use of the timeline editor, compression techniques, distribution and file formats, plug-ins and effects. Artists, composers, and theorists relevant to the subjects will be presented to provide theoretical and critical context to the work. Students will put theoretical concepts in practice through a series of small studies leading up to a final project. Open-source software tools will be used in this course wherever possible. Open to non-majors.', 'FA', 'F'),
('f102f348-d433-465b-8a80-b8d797d56adb', 'Introduction to Creative Methodologies I', 'PANF1500', 3.0, 'The course explores technical and philosophical approaches to creativity and its manifestations through interdisciplinary modes of artistic production. While creativity may be seen as a variable concept across different fields and context, we see commonalities in its description as a process that is shaped and developed through the sociocultural contexts in which it appears. Therefore, this course provides an experimental platform for social agency, developing ideas and creative solutions that can become catalysts for social justice and radical digital citizenship. To compare and contrast production methodologies, motivations and intersections across the arts, sciences and humanities, the course juxtaposes research presentations and experiential activities from broad academic, non-academic and epistemological contexts. This decoupling of practices with traditional mindsets provides a foundation for the student to explore new ideas and processes that deviate or build upon established examples. Students will read widely from key media theorists and creative practitioners, and experience films, sound works and performances. At the same time, they engage in practices of ideation through software and/or hardware prototyping, embracing the ethos of "making" as "thinking in action". Through this, the student''s individual imagination and curiosity form supporting frameworks for exercising critical thinking and problem-solving in the contemporary arts. Classes consist of a blend of presentations on diverse art-technology histories, introductions to AMPD faculty and studios, experiential exercises centered on specific applications and environments, cursory portraits of practices through specific artists across a wide range of fields, and critiques of major projects that combine theoretical and pragmatic approaches. Open to non-majors.', 'FA', 'F'),
('e35cd0e9-28de-4303-a956-6f97aef42e4a', 'Introduction to Creative Methodologies II', 'PANF1510', 3.0, 'The course continues the technical and philosophical approaches to creativity and its manifestations across interdisciplinary modes of artistic production. The course engages students through a series of thematic investigations informed by current sociocultural contexts and explores different ways that creativity can manifest as social justice and agency. The course will build on a foundation of skills developed through PANF 1500 3.0 (formerly PANF 1100 3.0) and will reinforce this across to a range of new contexts such as prototype design for digital-analog forms of artistic production. Students work towards a final project presentation. Regular critiques and discussions encourage students to think critically about the roles of emerging digital tools and methods within their own lives, in artistic production, and society at large. Classes consist of a blend of presentations on diverse art-technology histories, immersions in AMPD faculty studios and other research-creation processes, experiential exercises centered on specific applications and environments, cursory portraits of practices through specific artists across a wide range of fields, and critiques of major projects that combine theoretical and pragmatic approaches. Open to non-majors. Pre-requisite: FA/PANF 1500 3.0 Introduction to Creative Practices I (formerly PANF 1100 3.0), or by permission of the instructor.', 'FA', 'W'),
('a9a5bf55-3649-4453-815d-44ab4af2cfdb', 'Skills for Success in the Arts', 'PANF1900', 3.0, 'This course focuses on improving the generic skills needed for career success in the arts as well as effective learning for academic achievement. Addressing real-life circumstances, case studies and personal goals students develop essential skills transferable to professions aligned with the arts and the creative industries. Essential skills for success include critical and analytical thinking; the ability to define and research a thesis, the ability to write clearly in various formats, the ability to work with others; the ability to be innovative, adaptable and accountable. Theory-based and experiential, this course provides opportunities to master strategies and practical skills in time management, research, retention, reading for academic purposes, note-taking and test-taking. Open to non-majors.', 'FA', 'F'),
('8e79b2fc-9cd7-43ad-bcf8-31c6da97013d', 'Skills for Success in the Arts', 'PANF1900', 3.0, 'This course focuses on improving the generic skills needed for career success in the arts as well as effective learning for academic achievement. Addressing real-life circumstances, case studies and personal goals students develop essential skills transferable to professions aligned with the arts and the creative industries. Essential skills for success include critical and analytical thinking; the ability to define and research a thesis, the ability to write clearly in various formats, the ability to work with others; the ability to be innovative, adaptable and accountable. Theory-based and experiential, this course provides opportunities to master strategies and practical skills in time management, research, retention, reading for academic purposes, note-taking and test-taking. Open to non-majors.', 'FA', 'W'),
('79789a73-59f0-4a91-bfff-68dca080f6bb', 'Tech Knowledge Training: Unreal Engine Introduction', 'PANF2100A', 1.0, 'In this micro-credit course, students will learn techniques for creative media production using specific software and incorporate previous software knowledge. These short intensive courses offer technical training in how to navigate application interfaces, create media, and incorporate into a wider production ecosystem. Open to non-majors.', 'FA', 'F'),
('ef38b509-5272-4917-baf3-8aa21a00e245', 'Tech Knowledge Training: Animation and VFX with Fusion', 'PANF2100B', 1.0, 'In this micro-credit course, students will learn techniques for creative media production using specific software. Each section of this course will be dedicated to a specific skill set including: 3D modelling, 2D animation, video editing, creative programming, web coding. The course is designed to allow students to build on previous software experience to ensure a thorough understanding of fundamentals, of situating the technique/software within a wider set of technologies, and of how to establish a longer self-learning journey through to more advanced techniques. Each course is designed to be taken over six 2-hour sessions. Open to non-majors.', 'FA', 'W'),
('7011eccb-216b-4d61-a051-63f3ac557048', 'Augmented Listening and Applied Sound', 'PANF2103', 3.0, 'This introductory interdisciplinary course examines how we ''listen'' to the world and to each other. It explores how to listen using creative practices, and how to ''augment'' listening using both traditional and emerging sound technologies in artistic, industry and community settings. This is a hybrid learning experience that includes engaging with historical and critical material on ''hearing'' and ''listening'' from across different areas, as well as undertaking practical creative work and experiments in audio production. It is open to all levels of experience. Students will work on improving their listening skills and then towards designing and implementing their own augmented listening experience that connects and supports their work in creative technologies or their other areas of study and/or provides a basis to moving on to further study in sound.', 'FA', 'F'),
('e6de763d-639f-4994-a110-f1ff068673c0', 'Research Creation: Processes and Practices for Artists', 'PANF2500', 3.0, 'Research Creation; Processes & Practices for Artist: is understood as an exploration of the complex intersection of theoretical investigation, art production & traditional research. Key feature of this approach is to blend multiple research strategies in the pursuit of output that cannot be predetermined. Grounded in cultural production, this approach has gained traction as a multi-disciplinary tool used by designers, academics, artist and scientists to address complex research questions through creativity & innovation. Students will be exposed to multiple approaches to research creation as a tool for academic exploration, while demonstrating a thorough grasp of the key concepts through the development & execution of course projects. The theoretical foundations of this course will be two-fold by providing a firm academic understanding of current research creation practices in academia and beyond. Through sourcing & investigation of scholarly articles & primary sources students will develop the ability to acutely frame research questions & develop applicable creative methodologies on which to base their research. Through a series of assignments and projects students are expected to develop an ability to evaluate the appropriateness of different approaches to artistic creation using well established ideas & techniques. As the course progresses, students will develop their own skills & techniques that will be applied in the execution of their own research creation project. This course will be a hybrid studio/seminar course where students are expected to develop skills as both researcher & artist. Weekly presentations by students and the course leader will build on the understanding of this approach, accumulating in final presentations of student projects. Visit to galleries, research laboratories, artist studios and presentation venues or other timely events will be incorporated into the course in order to augment student learning.', 'FA', 'F'),
('603ea678-e274-404e-9441-844a1f64646b', 'Mixed Media Animation', 'PANF2503', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'W'),
('2668b098-e998-4ea1-b27f-a19e69d29347', 'Electro-Acoustic Orchestra', 'PANF3070', 3.0, 'This course engages the Electro-Acoustic Orchestra (EAO) as a context for developing digital instruments for expressive sound synthesis and processing in performance. It provides an opportunity for aspiring electronic musicians to develop a new digital performance practice, or to expand an existing practice, in an ensemble performance situation. DATT, MUSI and PANF 3070/4070 sections all meet together in order to form the larger ensemble. The EAO engages in regular improvisation sessions as a means to further develop attentiveness to all aspects of sound including timbre, texture, blending noise/tone, and blending acoustic/electronic sources in space. Assignments focus on developing and refining versatile electro/acoustic instruments which are used weekly in this challenging performance context. Compositions drawn from the experimental and electroacoustic literature are performed, as are new pieces which draw on the EAO''s unique gestural conducting language. The orchestra will present their work via public performances, in collaboration with the instructor and invited guest artists. Open to non-majors. Course credit exclusion: PANF 3070, DATT 3070.', 'FA', 'F'),
('71f0f2cc-fdd5-4f12-bd39-799038e7492e', 'Ethics in the Arts: Creative Practice, Social Justice, and the Creative Industries', 'PANF3100', 3.0, 'This course will explore various ethical considerations and approaches to ethics in the intersection of creative practice, social justice, and the creative industries. Focus will be on the history of art and social change and the ethical responsibility of members of the creative industries to develop an ethical framework to drive their practices. Topics covered include ethical considerations in media communication, feminism, decolonization, racism, transhumanism, and new technologies (such as AI, surveillance, etc.) and how creative practice and the creative industries can be continually challenged and revised by diverse perspectives, including those of Black, Indigenous, Latinx, Asian, near and Middle Eastern, and perspectives by people of colour with intersectional attention to feminist, queer, trans, and critical disability studies, among others. At the end of the course students will learn how to apply this knowledge and implement an ethical framework in their own creative practices and organizations. Open to non-majors.', 'FA', 'F'),
('9373f99f-3520-40cf-9dab-38e276b30eec', 'Crew Credit: Fourth Year Capstone Projects', 'PANF3100A', 1.0, 'In this one-credit course, students will be able to contribute to Fourth Year Capstone Courses in AMPD by taking on a significant role on a live or virtual production or animation crew. These short intensive courses offer experience in creating media as part of a larger collaboration.', 'FA', 'F'),
('d06bad06-3343-4cf1-a65c-2528ed6fe2d6', 'Crew Credit: Fourth Year Capstone Projects', 'PANF3100A', 1.0, 'In this one-credit course, students will be able to contribute to Fourth Year Capstone Courses in AMPD by taking on a significant role on a live or virtual production or animation crew. These short intensive courses offer experience in creating media as part of a larger collaboration.', 'FA', 'W'),
('6d5b6918-e5ab-4b44-9f8a-9b76051d9d03', 'Virtual Cinematography: Blending Real and Digital Worlds', 'PANF3852', 3.0, 'Provides skills to create new cinematic experiences and workflows by experimenting with game engines, 3D software, real and virtual cameras, real and virtual sets. This course will focus on building production workflows that integrate a wide variety of content and explore the orchestration of virtual and physical camera, set, and performance. Previous experience working with 3D content creation tools is highly recommended. Enrollment by application.', 'FA', 'W'),
('6ede2544-9d43-4329-b45a-b71aee806bf3', 'Motion Capture: Performance and Interactivity', 'PANF3853', 3.0, 'Introduces basic concepts in performance capture (motion capture), motion tracking, facial mocap for cinematography, games, and live performance. Previous experience working with 3D content creation tools is highly recommended. Enrollment by application.', 'FA', 'W'),
('297dea94-be7d-47da-83a8-509bd1713414', 'Virtual Worldbuilding: Methods and Issues', 'PANF3854', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'F'),
('5470b58e-a868-4e2a-a05e-c309d41287c5', 'C4 Collaborative Project', 'PANF3999', 3.0, 'Second- and third-year undergraduate students, who are either new to C4 or have previously taken C4 Primer, work intensively over a semester on one large, complex, multi-disciplinary project, pitched by a single partner. Students are assigned to sub-teams and then managed by up to ten C4 alumni who are registered in the C4 Management program.', 'FA', 'F'),
('a953b48c-2e47-4502-af92-15fd9edbdeed', 'C4 Collaborative Project', 'PANF3999', 3.0, 'Second- and third-year undergraduate students, who are either new to C4 or have previously taken C4 Primer, work intensively over a semester on one large, complex, multi-disciplinary project, pitched by a single partner. Students are assigned to sub-teams and then managed by up to ten C4 alumni who are registered in the C4 Management program.', 'FA', 'W'),
('db4ed72d-f186-4f79-84c8-f029005224a7', 'Electro-Acoustic Orchestra', 'PANF4070', 3.0, 'This course engages the Electro-Acoustic Orchestra (EAO) as a context for extending digital instruments for expressive sound synthesis and processing that were developed in the first course in the sequence (DATT/MUSI/PANF 3070). It provides an opportunity for established EAO members to refine their digital performance practice by introducing more advanced features, such as real-time processing of acoustic players, interactive systems based on sound analysis, hybrid analog/digital systems, and virtual acoustics. DATT, MUSI and PANF 3070/4070 sections all meet together in order to form the larger ensemble. The EAO engages in regular improvisation sessions as a means to further develop attentiveness to all aspects of sound including timbre, texture, blending noise/tone, and blending acoustic/electronic sources in space. Assignments focus on refining these more advanced digital instruments so that students can flexibly meet the demands of performing in this challenging ensemble context. Compositions drawn from the experimental and electroacoustic literature are performed, as are new pieces which draw on the EAO''s unique gestural conducting language. The orchestra will present their work via public performances, in collaboration with the instructor and invited guest artists. Open to non-majors. Prerequisite: FA/MUSI 3070 3.00. Course credit exclusions: FA/DATT 4070, FA/PANF 4070.', 'FA', 'F'),
('a3c7ac17-5ad9-42b5-99f3-13c712a0cc6f', 'Interdisciplinary Arts Capstone Studio', 'PANF4990', 6.0, 'Interdisciplinary Arts Capstone Studio is the culminating course for the Integrative Arts major. This year-long course offers fourth-year students the opportunity to synthesize the creative techniques and critical thinking that they have developed into a self-driven larger interdisciplinary project, either individual or collaborative. The course will respond to student needs and interests, drawing from a shared language in interdisciplinarity, conceptual and practical frameworks, art history, and social justice. Students will develop essential skills in producing their own work for and with the public. They will also gain professional development skills through regular field trips and engagement with arts organizations in the greater Toronto area. Open to non-majors. Prerequisite: Fourth year standing (minimum of 84 credits earned) in any AMPD program, or permission of the instructor.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('da4dc610-66cb-4819-8d8d-b77de2e2b881', 'C4 Capstone Project', 'PANF4999', 6.0, 'This two-term course is a student-driven, collaborative, multi-disciplinary, and socially-engaged experience organized around the values of respect, inclusion, cooperation, innovation, and commitment. Teams of 5 to 10 students develop their own research-design projects to address complex challenges that are pitched by partners and supported by on-and off-campus mentors and specialists. Pre-requisite: FA/PANF 3999 3.0.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('cdf8c541-419c-4cf7-a2c2-a66bd3f58353', 'Introduction to Acting I', 'THEA1010', 3.0, 'Introduces the principles, processes and skills of acting for the stage. Students will rehearse and perform a variety of class presentations, and actively participate in warm-ups, theatre games and acting exercises. A practical studio course with a strong written component to the curriculum. Required of all first-year theatre majors. Open only to theatre majors. Corequisites: FA/THEA 1100 3.00 and FA/THEA 1200 6.00. Note: A minimum of two rehearsal hours per week may be required.', 'FA', 'F'),
('29f21749-3499-4d04-8295-dedd5b51aaea', 'Introduction to Acting II', 'THEA1011', 3.0, 'Continues the exploration of the principles, processes and skills of acting for the stage commenced in FA/THEA 1010 3.00, with a particular emphasis on basic script analysis and the rehearsing and performing of monologues and scenes. A practical studio course with is a strong written component to the curriculum. Open to theatre majors only. Pre-requisite: a minimum grade of ''C+'' in FA/THEA 1010 3.00, and/or permission of the department.', 'FA', 'W'),
('d6ff58b9-8384-484f-ba8d-42676ea6deef', 'Introduction to Stagecraft I', 'THEA1100', 3.0, 'Introduces the fundamentals and vocabulary of design, lighting and stagecraft (in any given year this could include sound, props, paint etc.). Corequisites: FA/THEA 1010 3.00, FA/THEA 1200 6.00. Course credit exclusion: FA/THEA 1100 6.00, FA/DANC 1270 3.00. Note: Open to theatre majors only or permission of the department. Note: Evening and/or weekend crew work on department productions is required, as well as lab hours.', 'FA', 'F'),
('12ee78b9-bffe-4473-a4ab-0b71bdceb65f', 'Introduction to Production Practicum', 'THEA1101', 3.0, 'This is a new course to make production practicum (The Experiential Education cornerstone of the BFA in Production/Design) available to first year students who are accepted to the Theatre Department with a production portfolio and a stated intent to join the BFA in Production/Design or who demonstrate aptitude though their fall term crew assignments in Stage Craft 1. This will allow Production Practicum to be offered to all years of theatre educa-tion in the Department. Production Practicum courses allow students to work on professionally modelled theatre productions with professional directors, Faculty, designers, choreographers, and technical staff. Students experience hands-on, practical, dynamic theatre challenges in these student-led productions guided by professional mentors and advisors. This is a practical course in theatre production. The experiential learning in this course focuses on advanced skills in theatre production and technical work scaled to the student''s year of study and experience. As expected with the mounting of a full-scale piece of theatre, a significant time commitment is associated with Production Practicum courses beyond weekly class meetings, including standard crew hours and meetings. Prerequisites: by permission of the Production/Design Area. Co-requisite: At least one other studio course in theatre production. By permission only. By permission of the Department.', 'FA', 'W'),
('291e6e9f-1ecc-4b0e-b2c8-f6f3ba209eec', 'Introduction to Production Practicum', 'THEA1101', 3.0, 'This is a new course to make production practicum (The Experiential Education cornerstone of the BFA in Production/Design) available to first year students who are accepted to the Theatre Department with a production portfolio and a stated intent to join the BFA in Production/Design or who demonstrate aptitude though their fall term crew assignments in Stage Craft 1. This will allow Production Practicum to be offered to all years of theatre educa-tion in the Department. Production Practicum courses allow students to work on professionally modelled theatre productions with professional directors, Faculty, designers, choreographers, and technical staff. Students experience hands-on, practical, dynamic theatre challenges in these student-led productions guided by professional mentors and advisors. This is a practical course in theatre production. The experiential learning in this course focuses on advanced skills in theatre production and technical work scaled to the student''s year of study and experience. As expected with the mounting of a full-scale piece of theatre, a significant time commitment is associated with Production Practicum courses beyond weekly class meetings, including standard crew hours and meetings. Prerequisites: by permission of the Production/Design Area. Co-requisite: At least one other studio course in theatre production. By permission only. By permission of the Department.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('4ada5bf3-8d67-4e8c-a46b-dedbf5bf995a', 'Introduction to Stagecraft II', 'THEA1110', 3.0, 'Continues the introduction to the basics of theatrical production begun in FA/THEA 1100 3.00. Previously offered as: FA/THEA 1100 6.00. Note: Open to theatre majors only or permission of the department.', 'FA', 'W'),
('9a55e2ac-a9c7-4872-81ae-957bbcb6b6e4', 'Introduction to Theatre and Performance', 'THEA1200', 6.0, 'Designed to introduce Theatre students to the key concepts and debates animating Theatre Studies today. Beginning with the overarching question ""What is theatre and why/how do we study it?"" students will explore a wide range of topics, including (but not limited to) audience, space, time, bodies, community, spectacle, history, and politics. The course will also introduce students to a range of theoretical and methodological approaches for studying theatre and performance, e.g. performance ethnography, theatre historiography, community activism, practice-based research, educational outreach. Students will have opportunities to work individually as well as collaboratively on written and performance projects. They will also become familiar with Toronto''s lively theatre and performance ecology through visits to a wide range of performances over the course of the year. Corequisite: FA/THEA 1010 3.00, FA/THEA 1100 3.00.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('32ec62f0-229a-4a2a-9c9b-8f993244b899', 'Independent Production Practicum', 'THEA2000', 6.0, 'Provides an introductory practicum in production technique usually based on training and experience with a professional theatre. Prerequisite: By permission of the department.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('2661913b-dbfb-441c-88de-534c035c2af9', 'Independent Production Practicum', 'THEA2000', 3.0, 'Provides an introductory practicum in production technique usually based on training and experience with a professional theatre. Prerequisite: By permission of the department.', 'FA', 'W'),
('65fcc85f-265d-4685-a67c-bca4563d0291', 'Voice: Breath, Sound, Text', 'THEA2010', 3.0, 'Introduces the rudiments of voice training for the actor with an exploration of breath awareness, vocal sounding, and the personalization of imagistic poetry; introduces basic vocal anatomy and physiology; and begins an investigation of storytelling for the actor. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 1010 3.00, FA/THEA 1100 3.00, FA/THEA 1200 6.00. Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 1011 3.00 and/or audition plus permission of the department. Corequisites: FA/THEA 2020 3.00, FA/THEA 2030 3.00. Open to theatre majors only.', 'FA', 'F'),
('c3013a4c-f48a-4f06-8cee-0b3221d37823', 'Voice: Breath, Sound, Text', 'THEA2010', 3.0, 'Introduces the rudiments of voice training for the actor with an exploration of breath awareness, vocal sounding, and the personalization of imagistic poetry; introduces basic vocal anatomy and physiology; and begins an investigation of storytelling for the actor. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 1010 3.00, FA/THEA 1100 3.00, FA/THEA 1200 6.00. Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 1011 3.00 and/or audition plus permission of the department. Corequisites: FA/THEA 2020 3.00, FA/THEA 2030 3.00. Open to theatre majors only.', 'FA', 'W'),
('600f80cd-c011-43f0-8264-2ca59ded753b', 'Acting & Devising', 'THEA2020', 3.0, 'Introduces acting techniques through group and individual character building, with a focus on situational devised and improvisational storytelling. There is a strong emphasis on combining voice, movement and thought, through embodiment. Script analysis and research methods are explored. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 1010 3.00, FA/THEA 1011 3.00, FA/THEA 1100 3.00, FA/THEA 1200 6.00 and/or audition plus permission of the department. Grade of B or better in THEA 1011 3.00 or permission of the department. Corequisites: FA/THEA 2010 3.00 and FA/THEA 2020 3.00. Open to majors only.', 'FA', 'F'),
('f748887d-492c-4ba8-aa9c-2890510fa324', 'Acting: Scene Study', 'THEA2021', 3.0, 'Furthers script analysis using previously published plays that challenge and strengthen the students'' learning path. Emphasis is placed more upon character objectives, managing character emotions, and continuing the student''s overall embodiment while applying tactics to any given character role. Prerequisites: Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 2020 3.00 or permission of the department. Open to theatre majors only.', 'FA', 'W'),
('ca0cfb10-1e56-4def-baeb-dcea3936b8b2', 'Scene Study', 'THEA2022A', 3.0, 'Offers a survey course that is a basic exploration of the acting process. Students face the challenge of translating a scene from script to stage as performers and discover the questions that must be answered before the scene can live. This course is restricted to Performance Creation students. Prerequisites: FA/THEA1010 3.00 (a minimum grade of ''C+''), FA/THEA1100 3.00 and FA/THEA1200 6.00 and/or permission of the department.', 'FA', 'F'),
('177279eb-61be-4836-a504-82afd31ebca7', 'Movement: Breath, Strength, Ease', 'THEA2030', 3.0, 'An introduction to foundational movement techniques necessary for the physical actor. This course will investigate the integrated connection of movement to breath, while building strength and physical organization. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 1010 3.00, FA/THEA 1011 3.00, FA/THEA 1100 3.00 and FA/THEA 1200 6.00 and/or audition plus permission of the department. Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 1011 3.00 or permission of the department. Corequisites: FA/THEA 2010 3.00 and FA/THEA 2020 3.00. Open to majors only.', 'FA', 'F'),
('d1af9bdf-4f19-4276-87b4-e091fe61c36f', 'Movement: Breath, Strength, Ease', 'THEA2030', 3.0, 'An introduction to foundational movement techniques necessary for the physical actor. This course will investigate the integrated connection of movement to breath, while building strength and physical organization. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 1010 3.00, FA/THEA 1011 3.00, FA/THEA 1100 3.00 and FA/THEA 1200 6.00 and/or audition plus permission of the department. Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 1011 3.00 or permission of the department. Corequisites: FA/THEA 2010 3.00 and FA/THEA 2020 3.00. Open to majors only.', 'FA', 'W'),
('85816309-9dfa-417e-8231-2aee8a1bf673', 'Introduction to Devised Theatre', 'THEA2051', 3.0, 'Offers students an introduction to the devising of theatre in all aspects, from conception to execution. Classroom exercises and performance project assignments will allow students to explore the processes and skills of the theatre practitioner. Practical studio course. Prerequisite: Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 2020 3.00, FA/THEA 2050 3.00, or FA/THEA 2141 3.00, or permission of the department. Open to theatre majors only. Note: Plus rehearsal hours.', 'FA', 'W'),
('61d68aeb-584a-4543-be8f-2da0fdf31910', 'Voice and Speech I', 'THEA2060', 3.0, 'An introductory course in voice and speech for effective communication. Exercises and practical applications in a variety of speaking contexts focus on vocal function, breath, resonance, articulation and coping with performance stress.', 'FA', 'F'),
('326b64fd-43af-402d-984b-e7d28bd90de8', 'Voice and Speech I', 'THEA2060', 3.0, 'An introductory course in voice and speech for effective communication. Exercises and practical applications in a variety of speaking contexts focus on vocal function, breath, resonance, articulation and coping with performance stress.', 'FA', 'W'),
('8a9a5aa2-c4fe-4a8e-84bc-f78e17829d4a', 'Production Practicum', 'THEA2100', 3.0, 'Provides students with the opportunity to learn and practice the technical and management skills associated with creating a production. Required of all second-year students working in production and design. Open to majors/minors. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 1010 3.00, FA/THEA 1100 3.00 with a minimum grade of C+ or by permission of the Theatre Department. Corequisite: FA/THEA 2110 6.00.', 'FA', 'W'),
('b7778629-c561-4134-b6b9-06dfee14fa4c', 'Production Practicum', 'THEA2103', 3.0, 'Provides second-year production students with the opportunity to expand their learning and practice of technical and management skills associated with creating a production. Taken in conjunction with FA/THEA 2100 3.00 this course is open to second-year students working in production and design who want to work on more than one project in their second-year. Open to majors/minors. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 1010 3.00, FA/THEA 1100 3.00 with a minimum grade of C+ or by permission of the Theatre Department. Corequisites: FA/THEA 2110 6.00.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('c365655a-a75c-4a1f-8698-a0dc01e4ca2b', 'Production Practicum', 'THEA2106', 6.0, 'Provides second year production students with the opportunity to expand their learning and practice of technical and management skills associated with creating a production. An alternative to 2100 3.0 for all second-year students working in production and design who want to work on more than one project in their second year Open to majors/minors. Prerequisite: FA/THEA 1010 3.00, FA/THEA 1100 3.00 with a minimum grade of C+ or by permission of the Theatre Department. Corequisite: FA/THEA 2110 6.00, FA/THEA 2410 6.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('6354f33d-d4c3-403b-833b-eb26c498979a', 'Production Practicum', 'THEA2109', 9.0, 'Provides second year production students with the opportunity to expand their learning and practice of technical and management skills associated with creating a production. An alternative to 2100 3.0 for all second-year students working in production and design who want to work on more than one project in their second year Open to majors/minors. Prerequisite: FA/THEA 1010 3.00, FA/THEA 1100 3.00 with a minimum grade of C+ or by permission of the Theatre Department. Corequisite: FA/THEA 2110 6.00, FA/THEA 2410 6.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('fabf0c8a-3582-4f58-b589-86e741d5262e', '2nd year Stagecraft', 'THEA2110', 6.0, 'Offers further practical exploration into the elements of theatrical production. Students will explore management, scenic, drafting, lighting and sound techniques. Required of all second-year students working in production and design. Open to theatre majors/minors or permission of the department. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 1010 3.00, FA/THEA 1100 3.00 with a minimum grade of C+, FA/THEA 1200 6.00 and permission of the Department.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('1ae8f397-4657-428f-ab2c-ea9a3fb4e544', 'Introduction to Costuming for Performance', 'THEA2120', 3.0, 'Introduction to Costuming for Performance equips the student with a comprehensive understanding of the varied disciplines required in the costume realization process. It examines techniques from conception to final costume fabrication and how costumes are integral to performance. The course focuses on the practical skills needed for managing a costume shop, pattern drafting, costume construction, and maintenance. By learning the proper use of the equipment and employing costuming techniques, students will be well-prepared to continue their work in this field. Open to majors and non-majors. Studio Course meets 6 hours a week for the fall term.', 'FA', 'F'),
('cd39bd48-6b77-4790-a66b-26ca921b6bbc', 'Introduction to Costuming for Performance 2', 'THEA2121', 3.0, 'Introduction to Costuming for Performance 2 continues the work begun in FA/THEA 2120 3.00, further exploring the costuming process and equipping the student with a comprehensive understanding of the varied disciplines required in the costume realization process. It examines techniques from conception to final costume fabrication and how costumes are integral to performance. The course focuses on the practical skills needed for managing a costume shop and the crafts of costume construction. By learning the proper use of the equipment and employing costuming techniques, students will be well-prepared to continue their work in this field. Open to majors and non-majors. Studio Course meets 6 hours a week for the winter term.', 'FA', 'W'),
('b6763f94-dd80-411a-a313-71afc5d147b2', 'Introduction to Sculpture: Soft Sculpture', 'THEA2122', 3.0, 'This Experiential Education course is designed to engage students in creating three-dimensional, non-wearable sculpture in soft materials such as textiles, flexible foam and paper. Through a combination of presentations and hands-on studio projects, this course will explore soft materials as a sculptural medium, and contextualize the use of soft materials in both historical and contemporary frameworks of artistic production, emphasizing individual, process-based approaches to creative problem solving. The sculptural processes surrounding fabrication of soft sculptures introduces students to a range of sculptural methodologies and skills employing pattern drafting, assembly, and material manipulation. Through in-class demonstrations students are introduced to a range of skills and tools to be utilized in the production of their studio projects. Assignments consist of a series of projects exploring different working processes and sculptural methodologies. In addition to these projects, in-class discussions, technical assignments and readings engage students in the dialogue surrounding contemporary sculptural concerns such as form, process and spatial awareness. Craftsmanship and a demonstrated knowledge of materials are a major factor in this course. In order for students to be able to fully articulate their idea in a given material, they must understand the limits and possibilities inherent in the fabrication process. It is expected that each student will develop a degree of sophistication and competence when working with these material processes. Lectures, demonstrations, group critiques and hands-on instruction are the essential means by which we will investigate sculptural problem-solving. The class participates in material specific demonstrations, technical assignments and class critiques of finished work. Periodically there will be in-class work periods, but it is expected that the majority of the assignments will be completed outside of class time.', 'FA', 'W'),
('613b8d54-bec2-431b-aea4-15bbbbda5a55', 'Introduction to Design for Performance I', 'THEA2141', 3.0, 'This class is an introduction to how theatrical design areas (which include set, costumes, light, sound, and projection) relate to storytelling and enhance an audience''s connection and experience of the play. This form of non-verbal communication provides a mirror to our own cultural perspectives: how and why we see and experience the things we do when reading a script or translating the text into production. The course will examine different ways a designer approaches and deconstructs a theme in a script through references and research and how that translates to a final design. The elements and principles of design will be introduced and applied to assignments. These foundational design tools are transferable to many performance and entertainment disciplines, such as screen, dance, and musical performance. The course will include visiting guest speakers and may include off-campus site visits to view live performances. Open to majors and non-majors. Course Type: Studio delivery 3hs synchronous in studio, 3hs asynchronous per week for 12 weeks', 'FA', 'F'),
('1f9d1a24-8812-4cff-af01-fd0c7f0a63f3', 'History of Theatre and Performance', 'THEA2200', 6.0, 'Offers an historical survey of theatre and performance from classical Athens to late C20. It examines theatre and performance in the larger social, cultural and political contexts by integrating critical analysis with explorations of various performance possibilities. Required of all second-year majors. Open to non-majors. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 1010 3.00, FA/THEA 1100 3.00 and FA/THEA 1200 6.00 or permission of the Instructor.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('2864d35f-95cb-4bfc-958d-30d1972858a8', 'Theatre and Social Justice', 'THEA2210', 3.0, 'Provides a basis for continuing work in theatre studies. This course examines questions about the nature of theatre and its role in society. Required of all second-year theatre studies students. Open to non-majors. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 1010 3.00, FA/THEA 1100 3.00 and FA/THEA 1200 6.00 or permission of the Instructor. Corequisite: For theatre majors, FA/THEA 2200 6.00.', 'FA', 'F'),
('773da1ea-262a-4719-b15a-127657616cba', 'Visual History for Stage and Screen', 'THEA2410', 6.0, 'Charts a survey course through a visual history of human civilization''s architecture, clothing, interior design, and furnishings, from the beginnings of civilization to the present day. There is a strong focus on honing research skills of primary visual sources. This course is a foundation for set, costume, and lighting designers of all types of live performance and media. We must know the rules before we can break them. Required of all theatre production students.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('077f9171-9502-4d4d-90e7-5c65c0f3e30b', 'Time-Based Art: Crossing Boundaries', 'THEA2700', 3.0, 'Introduces students to interdisciplinary studio practice, emphasizing critical thinking and creative exploration of time and process. Sound, video and performance are investigated. Technical workshops include introductions to sound and video editing, audio recorder and microphone use. This course is cross-listed between the Department of Visual Arts and the Theatre Department.', 'FA', 'F'),
('655de3a3-d014-47e3-8aee-b6b770ea01cc', 'Time-Based Art: Performance Art and Everyday Life', 'THEA2701', 3.0, 'Focuses on individual and group projects plus readings on time-based media, especially contemporary performance art. Students participate in exercises that promote original responses to the everyday environment, and are encouraged to use a variety of media in making works. This course is cross-listed between the Department of Visual Arts and the Theatre Department.', 'FA', 'W'),
('4ad72bf2-4473-4c3a-8410-2dc93cc8eec7', 'Independent Production Practicum', 'THEA3000', 3.0, 'Provides an intermediate practicum in production technique usually based on training and experience with a professional theatre/organization. Prerequisite: Permission of the Theatre Department. Open to theatre majors and minors only.', 'FA', 'W'),
('4a82163f-abaf-4691-92ca-335738d7a149', 'Independent Production Practicum', 'THEA3000', 3.0, 'Provides an intermediate practicum in production technique usually based on training and experience with a professional theatre/organization. Prerequisite: Permission of the Theatre Department. Open to theatre majors and minors only.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('ed996073-1c36-4f66-952d-35e98be48e27', 'Voice: Classical Text & Intelligibility', 'THEA3010', 3.0, 'A continuation of voice, speech, and text training principles and methodologies, including an examination of Canadian & American speech patterns and intelligibility for the stage. Exploration of Shakespeare text and Elizabethan rhetoric is used to research, personalize, and embody the dense meaning of complex language. Prerequisites: grade of B or better in FA/THEA 2010 3.00, or permission of the department. Open to majors only.', 'FA', 'F'),
('d734e547-341d-4b3b-9f24-52d318c69ab4', 'Voice: Speech & Dialect', 'THEA3011', 3.0, 'A continuation of voice, speech, and text training principles and methodologies, including an examination of the sounds of the world''s languages, and the use of phonetics for the actor to describe those sounds. An exploration of accents of English is used to develop speech skills for transformational acting through the application to appropriate texts. Prerequisites: grade of B or better in FA/THEA 2010 3.00 or permission of the department. Open to majors only.', 'FA', 'W'),
('463b1c73-b032-43d2-ab10-8a0e1224de50', 'Acting: The Modern and Shakespearean Canons', 'THEA3020', 3.0, 'Continued exploration of acting techniques, character development and play/text analysis begun in FA/THEA 2021 3.00, and a contemporary examination of theatrical canons that have influenced the development of Canadian Theatre. Prerequisites: Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 2021 3.00 or permission of the department. Open to majors only.', 'FA', 'F'),
('e4f2c90a-20cf-44aa-9568-7ee9e3d60acb', 'Acting: The Play', 'THEA3021', 3.0, 'FA/THEA 3021 continues the application and deepening of acting techniques, approaches to character development and text/play analysis through the in-class staging of a full play text. Prerequisites: Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 3020 3.00 or permission of the department. Corequisites: FA/THEA 3011 3.00 and FA/THEA 3031 3.00. Open to majors only.', 'FA', 'W'),
('99d30a51-02ae-4357-88eb-f11d3b3de7bc', 'Movement: Physical Range, Character Body, & Transformation', 'THEA3030', 3.0, 'This intermediate level course is a continuation of techniques, principles and explorations of movement for the actor 2030. The course will help free up the body to play physically dynamic and varied characters and generally increase the students physical range for acting, using play, body language principles and mimicry. Prerequisites: Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 2030 3.00 or permission of the department. Open to majors only.', 'FA', 'F'),
('0fbbe42a-1c32-43a5-ace4-3c8b2aaaa84e', 'Movement: Emotional Authenticity-Body, Voice & Acting', 'THEA3031', 3.0, 'This course focuses on physical approaches to acting that include vocal work to increase emotional range and authenticity. The class integrates rigorous vocal and physical training that teaches the student to use physical awareness to access emotional expression necessary for acting. Prerequisites: Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 2030 3.00 or permission of the department. Open to majors only.', 'FA', 'W'),
('6b3ef729-6079-43bc-9dae-ca53937a48c5', 'Introduction to Clown and Mask for Performers', 'THEA3040', 3.0, 'Designed to introduce the principles of dynamic whole-body performance through play and improvisation based in mask and clown work. A variety of clown styles and techniques will be studied, such as stage clown, Pochinko clown, bouffon and baby clown. The course will also cover an introduction to neutral mask, as well as character mask. A simple mask will be made in class. This class will assist the actor to find freedom and the courage to go beyond naturalism, with authenticity and spontaneity. By assisting the performer to free up their impulses, students will build new and varied characters and find a playful approach to acting in general. Prerequisites: Grade of B or better in FA/THEA2021 3.00 or FA/THEA 2051 3.00, and FA/THEA2030 and FA/THEA2010, and/or permission of the department. Open to majors only.', 'FA', 'F'),
('0652e20e-0302-4e97-9a6f-d6acb15ce277', 'An Introduction to Motion Capture Performance for Actors', 'THEA3041', 3.0, 'Introduces the performer to the world of motion capture (mocap), using both practical and creative approaches. Utilizing their previous Actor training, students will learn: to perform in a digital environment; the importance of creating fully-realized scenes using only their imagination and a script; to live and play in imagined worlds using few (or no) props, set pieces, or costumes; and the requisite technical requirements for mocap performance, many of which are uncommon to other acting genres. Students work on scenes from professional AAA video games and will be encouraged to heighten character stakes and rediscover the freedom to play, while keeping their work authentic and organic. Collaboration and constructive feedback from peers is expected, and crucial to developing an understanding of this craft. Prerequisites: Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 2021 or FA/THEA 2051, and FA/THEA 2030 and FA/THEA 2010, and/or permission of the department. Open to majors only.', 'FA', 'W'),
('6d185f21-c88d-4687-9550-d018874ee002', 'Devised Theatre in Practice I', 'THEA3050', 3.0, 'Rigorously develops the processes and skills of devising and interpreting theatre works within a collaborative context. In classroom exercises, and through the rehearsal and performance of assigned performance projects, students will explore and practice the creative approaches of the theatre practitioner. Practical studio course. Prerequisites: Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 2051 3.00, and permission of the department. Open to majors only. Note: Plus rehearsal hours. Corequisite: FA/THEA 3200 3.00.', 'FA', 'F'),
('800193dd-4390-4537-b4d1-c7dd7193e237', 'Devised Theatre in Practice II', 'THEA3051', 3.0, 'Building on the processes and skills investigated in FA/THEA 3050 3.00, this practical studio course offers students further opportunities to practice the creation and presentation of devised theatre pieces in a collaborative setting. Classroom exercises and assigned performance projects will prepare participants for their final year public showcase in FA/THEA 4050 3.00 and FA/THEA 4051 3.00. Prerequisites: Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 3050 3.00 and permission of the Theatre Department. Open to majors only. Note: Plus rehearsal hours.', 'FA', 'W'),
('89ec6c1e-39ab-4f60-b723-f88597fd5144', 'Public Speaking:Prep,Practice,Present', 'THEA3060', 3.0, 'Examines how to prepare and practice for presentations in seminars, office, public events. Develops physical, vocal and rhetorical skills needed for public presentations. Prerequisite: third- or fourth- year standing, FA/THEA 2060.3.00 or permission of the department. Open to theatre majors and non-majors.', 'FA', 'F'),
('808e0413-58d2-4517-80f8-6dcd1cf3c0d0', 'Performance Practicum I', 'THEA3070', 3.0, 'Intended for third-year students working in acting, or in assistant directing mounted or unmounted department productions in the fall term. This course offers an intensive opportunity to develop and improve skills in the creation of workshop productions. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 2021 3.00 or FA/THEA 2051 3.00 and/or permission of the department. Open to majors only.', 'FA', 'F'),
('e6a7ca70-4941-4959-ab65-920dbef836ee', 'Performance Practicum II', 'THEA3071', 3.0, 'Intended for third-year students in acting, or in assistant directing mounted or unmounted department productions in the winter term. This course offers an intensive opportunity to develop and improve skills in the creation of workshop productions. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 3020 3.00 or FA/THEA 3050 3.00 and/or permission of the department. Open to majors only.', 'FA', 'W'),
('baafb1d4-6812-4b84-8310-b9b0a126dfaf', 'Production Practicum', 'THEA3100', 3.0, 'Required of all third-year students working in production and design. The course gives students an opportunity to experience all aspects of creating a show (including stage management) from a production standpoint. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 2100 or FA/THEA 2101 with a minimum grade of C+ or by permission of the Production/Design Area. Corequisite: At least one other studio course in theatre production. Note: Students may take FA/THEA 3100 3.00 or FA/THEA 3101 6.00, but not both concurrently. Open to majors and minors only or by permission of the department.', 'FA', 'W'),
('85439a65-da47-4fb6-8200-9bebf1375c40', 'Production Practicum', 'THEA3103', 3.0, 'Required of all third-year students working in production and design. The course gives students an opportunity to experience all aspects of creating a show (including stage management) from a production standpoint. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 2100 or FA/THEA 2101 or FA/THEA 2103/6/9 with a minimum grade of C+ or by permission of the Production/Design Area. Corequisite: At least one other studio course in theatre production. Note: Students may take FA/THEA 3100 3.00 or FA/THEA 3106 6.00, but not both concurrently. Note: Open to majors and minors only or by permission of the department.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('b514683a-6cfb-4c9a-b2d3-1c63709f422a', 'Production Practicum', 'THEA3106', 6.0, 'Required of all third-year students working in production and design. The course gives students an opportunity to experience all aspects of creating a show (including stage management) from a production standpoint. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 2100 or FA/THEA 2101 or FA/THEA 2303/6/9 with a minimum grade of C+ or by permission of the Production/Design Area. Corequisite: At least one other studio course in theatre production. Students may take FA/THEA 3100 3.00 or FA/THEA 3106 6.00, but not both concurrently. Open to majors and minors only or by permission of the department.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('7320b94f-f2c3-4a2c-8fc5-1078c14c873f', 'Production Practicum', 'THEA3109', 9.0, 'Required of all third-year students working in production and design. The course gives students an opportunity to experience all aspects of creating a show (including stage management) from a production standpoint. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 2100 or FA/THEA 2101 or FA/THEA 2103/6/9 with a minimum grade of C+ or by permission of the Production/Design Area. Corequisite: At least one other studio course in theatre production. Note: Students may take FA/THEA 3100 or FA/THEA 3109, but not both concurrently. Open to majors and minors only or by permission of the department.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('78534920-6ed4-47d2-b01e-5619e892dd95', 'Props and Paint for Performance and Cosplay', 'THEA3110', 3.0, 'Explores the principles and practice of producing properties and scenic art for performance and Cosplay. Course credit exclusion: FA/THEA 3171 3.00. Studio course meets 3 hours a week for the year.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('bbb48452-ee12-40ac-b7b3-56ed1da250da', 'Costuming for Performance', 'THEA3120', 3.0, 'This course is designed to provide an intensive application and further development of work begun in FA/THEA 2120 3.00. The course''s primary focus is on the exciting and practical skills of costume drafting, construction, and the proper use of equipment and costume materials for theatre, dance, screen, and cosplay. Prerequisite: FA/THEA 2120 3.00 or permission from Department of Theatre, Dance and Performance. Studio Course that meets 3 hours a week for the full year.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('0ba6d92a-5059-4e19-9bdc-dacf945290a1', 'Lighting Design I', 'THEA3130', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'F'),
('a023a20b-d195-4033-b310-40dc699da1d1', 'Lighting Design II', 'THEA3131', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'W'),
('00ecb8b0-7fbb-4ad7-9208-227c90c92c93', 'Technology in Arts Management', 'THEA3135', 3.0, 'Trains students to use current technologies to mange both production and their own careers. Students will be introduced to the fundamental concepts and uses of many computer programs and protocols that will be helpful for furthering themselves in their professions. Students will learn the basic skills needed to establish a web presence for information distribution and self promotion, familiarize themselves with best practices and operation of databases to better manage and record information, and work with common graphic design and presentation software to create more effective and clear documentation.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('d11d877f-5686-44fb-98eb-a62a676fb04d', 'Set and Costume Design for Performance II', 'THEA3143', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'F'),
('621bf4dd-7a33-4259-9156-fe167f3169ce', 'Set and Costume Design for Performance III', 'THEA3144', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'W'),
('b08a81d1-1f63-4b15-b8ad-938becead946', 'Scenic Drafting for Stage and Screen', 'THEA3145', 3.0, 'This blended lecture and online course familiarizes students with technical drawing for the stage and screen for a technician and designer alike. This involves the ability not only to communicate technical material for a design''s realization, but also to translate renderings and/or models into technical drawings for costing and construction. This course is suitable for both students of stage scenography and screen based media production. Course type: Studio hybrid on-line 3hrs/week for 12 weeks', 'FA', 'F'),
('2e6e6e16-d217-4cc5-8906-3bb4086ab91f', 'Stage Management', 'THEA3180', 3.0, 'A full year course with in-person and asynchronous seminars and labs in the fall term and a focus on practical experiential learning in the winter term. The course is practicum in the art of stage management, consisting of lectures and seminars on theory, methodology and organization. Focused is on practical application and development of skills. Fall term will be classroom and studio focused meeting weekly during the scheduled class time. The winter term will focus on practical skills though experiential learning opportunities like community-focused learning opportunities, and work-focused practicums. There be limited in-person meetings during the scheduled class time. Students are expected to work outside of class time on shows, events, films, and/or job shadows and report and reflect on their experiences. (40-50 hours). Travel off campus may be required. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 2106 6.00 or permission of the Department.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('58947fe4-d304-4484-b1ca-dc6ad3af1876', 'Canadian Theatre and Performance', 'THEA3200', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'W'),
('befa250f-fdec-4ee1-a3c2-5d626a00bc7a', 'Screen Acting: Practical Approaches to the Craft of the Actor', 'THEA3250', 3.0, 'Offers an introduction to the complexities of the actor''s process as it relates to contemporary film and television production. This course is open to all Arts, Media, Performance & Design students and theatre majors.', 'FA', 'W'),
('4bae4575-919b-45df-9e5b-926c700fa0f6', 'Independent Production Practicum', 'THEA4000', 3.0, 'Provides An advanced practicum in production technique usually based on training and experience with a professional theatre. Prerequisite: Permission of the production area, by special arrangement. Open only to theatre majors.', 'FA', 'W'),
('d0af2e32-e5ff-4c84-a0b3-eb182f4e9c5e', 'Independent Production Practicum', 'THEA4000', 3.0, 'Provides An advanced practicum in production technique usually based on training and experience with a professional theatre. Prerequisite: Permission of the production area, by special arrangement. Open only to theatre majors.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('9fbb3dc5-d615-463c-8dd1-b9fd55282a1c', 'Voice: Accent & Voice-Over', 'THEA4010', 3.0, 'A continuation of voice, speech, and text training principles and methodologies, including an examination of different vocal levels for stage, screen and voice-over. Further exploration of accents, and the introduction of on-mic technique for a range of voice-over styles. Prerequisites: Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 2010 3.00 or permission of the department. Open to majors only.', 'FA', 'F'),
('cf2bd69b-5012-4c78-b8d9-82cca3619f25', 'Acting: Audition & Showcase Preparation', 'THEA4020', 3.0, 'Acting: Audition and Showcase Preparation offers students an in-depth exploration of the tools needed for the selection, analysis, rehearsal, and performance of monologues, an essential career skill for the professional actor. Acting techniques will build on and further develop the work begun in the second and third years of the Acting program and will culminate in presenting material for Next Generation Showcase in Toronto. Prerequisites: Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 3021 3.00 or permission of the department. Open to majors only.', 'FA', 'F'),
('2fa1fa84-f265-4ea4-8633-71e080099b19', 'Acting for Camera', 'THEA4021', 3.0, 'Acting for Camera offers students an in-depth exploration of the tools needed to act in film and television, in addition to essential career skills for professional actors. Students will rehearse and present a variety of scenes for the camera, deepen their understanding of the acting process and auditioning through on-camera analysis, and develop skills to promote themselves in audition circumstances. Prerequisites: Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 4020 3.00 or permission of the department. Open to majors only.', 'FA', 'W'),
('5a44c377-2b41-4f59-bf97-d0045fe65b2c', 'Movement: Physical Performance Skills', 'THEA4030', 3.0, 'This movement course focuses on physical theatre creation. This class offers training in various approaches to physical improvisation as a tool to create theatre; both to devise compelling characters for scripted work as well as create your own performance material. Prerequisites: Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 3030 3.00 or FA/THEA 3031 3.00 or permission of the department. Open to majors only.', 'FA', 'F'),
('028fc3aa-fdbc-4727-89e1-87a3656b1dd1', 'Performance Styles of Acting', 'THEA4040', 3.0, 'This course introduces the performer to acting approaches for a variety of styles of Theatre. In tandem with the Performance Creation (PC) program, Acting & Performance students will focus on two styles of theatre, to further broaden their range of methods when working on both text and non-text-based performances. Styles to be studied may include, but are not limited to: Comedy, Musical Theatre, Feminist Theatre, Indigenous Theatre, Cultural Styles (such as Butoh), as well as Period Styles (such as Restoration, Melodrama, Ancient Greek or Roman Theatre). The styles will be chosen in tandem with the Performance Creation curriculum and the instructors'' skillsets from year to year. This course will culminate in presentations of scenes from the students'' work in each style for peers and a limited audience, to provide students with valuable feedback from the live nature of this craft. Prerequisites: Grade of B or better in THEA 3021 3.0 or THEA 3051 3.0 or permission of the department.', 'FA', 'F'),
('e46b55ec-5c2b-418b-b950-6711342e52e5', 'Devised Theatre Perspectives', 'THEA4050', 3.0, 'Offers students in their final year of Devised Theatre an intensive curriculum in further perspectives of devised theatre exploration. Incorporating skill sets from the previous two years of training, this class will focus on advanced techniques for creating and performing original theatre works in a group setting, in preparation for a performance in the Devised Theatre Festival in the winter term. Prerequisites: Grade of B or better in FA/THEA 3051 3.00 and permission of the Theatre Department. Open to majors only. Note: Plus rehearsal hours.', 'FA', 'F'),
('16842c76-0ec2-462f-aff8-e56f5c6d26fc', 'Devised Theatre Workshop', 'THEA4051', 3.0, 'Offers an opportunity for students to consolidate their training by creating and performing their own piece(s) for a public performance in the Devised Theatre Festival at the end of the winter term. In accordance with the self-reliant nature of this series of classes, students will participate fully in all aspects of the collaborative devising of theatre in these presentations, from conception to execution, with minimal dependence on external technical support. Prerequisites: Minimum of B in FA/THEA 4050 3.00 and permission of the Theatre Department. Open to majors only. Note: Plus rehearsal hours.', 'FA', 'W'),
('d9f2e58d-8914-4831-a820-064e5cff4fba', 'Performance Practicum III', 'THEA4070', 3.0, 'Intended for final-year students in acting, or in assistant directing mounted department productions in the fall term. This course gives students an intensive opportunity to develop advanced skills in the creation of a fully mounted production. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 3021 3.00 or FA/THEA 3050 3.00, and/or permission of the Theatre Department. For assistant directors, it is recommended to take FA/THEA 4485 3.00. Open to Theatre majors only.', 'FA', 'F'),
('27ac29e7-dbf2-4095-95ad-ac58e30ab5b5', 'Performance Practicum IV', 'THEA4071', 3.0, 'Intended for final-year students in acting, or in assistant directing mounted department productions in the winter term. This course gives students an intensive opportunity to develop advanced skills in the creation of a fully mounted production. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 4020 3.00 or FA/THEA 4050 3.00, and/or permission of the Theatre Department. Corequisites for Specialized Honours BFA Program (Acting) FA/THEA 4011 3.00 and FA/THEA 4021 3.00. For assistant directors, it is recommended to take FA/THEA 4485 3.00. Open to Theatre majors only.', 'FA', 'W'),
('8eefd16b-fedb-419a-931d-0ae4eed968fe', 'Production Practicum II', 'THEA4100', 3.0, 'Required of all fourth-year students working in production and design, the course gives advanced students an opportunity to further develop and perfect skills in the creation of a show. Labs include work on departmental productions. Prerequisite: FA/THEA 3100 3.00 or FA/THEA 3101 6.00 with a minimum grade of C+ or by permission of the Production/Design Area. Corequisite: At least one other 4000-level course in theatre production. Open to non-majors by permission of the department. Note: Students may take FA/THEA 4100 3.00 or FA/THEA4101 6.00, but not both concurrently.', 'FA', 'W'),
('6e78ad84-8074-46b4-9f01-38912bf3f003', 'Production Practicum II', 'THEA4103', 3.0, 'Required of all fourth-year students working in production and design, the course gives advanced students an opportunity to further develop and perfect skills in the creation of a show. Labs include work on departmental productions. Prerequisite: FA/THEA 3100 3.00 or FA/THEA 3101 3.00 or 6.00 or FA/THEA 3103 3.0 or FA/THEA 3106 6.0.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('f2279a48-69ce-48b5-bb08-735fee3820bd', 'Production Practicum II', 'THEA4106', 6.0, 'Required of all fourth-year students working in production and design, the course gives advanced students an opportunity to further develop and perfect skills in the creation of a show. Labs include work on departmental productions. Prerequisite: FA/THEA 3100 3.0 or FA/THEA 3101 6.0 or FA/THEA 3106 6.0.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('1ebc4f58-7938-446e-88c3-45b21198ff83', 'Production Practicum II', 'THEA4109', 9.0, 'Required of all fourth-year students working in production and design, the course gives advanced students an opportunity to further develop and perfect skills in the creation of a show. Labs include work on departmental productions. Prerequisite: FA/THEA 3100 or FA/THEA 3101 or FA/THEA 3103, 3106, or 3109.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('2a27b1ef-01c5-4f4d-abd1-b226cd87e941', 'Sustainable Staging Techniques', 'THEA4111', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'W'),
('dfb07cbb-2b5f-4f5b-9117-745e96e60464', 'Exploring Cosplay through Props and Costume', 'THEA4120', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'Y'),
('7c820534-d1e7-48f4-9877-b59fbd8e8e52', 'Design for Performance IV', 'THEA4146', 6.0, 'This advanced theatrical set, costume, lighting, sound, and video design course will focus on further honing technical design skills and learning to communicate with directors and technicians to understand how to realize designs. At this point students should be well versed in the elements and principles of design and how to use those to create strong conceptual design ideas inspired by text/script and how to navigate the design process. Students may have the opportunity to collaborate with a student director to conceive and execute a design from the beginning of the process to the first day of rehearsal. Course will include visiting guest speakers and trips to live performance. Prerequisites: FA/THEA2141 Introduction to Design for Performance I. Open to Theatre majors, minors and non-majors. Enrolment by application and permission of the course director. Open to non-majors? (Y/N): Y', 'FA', 'Y'),
('5a4f35bc-edf0-4dfc-a7ad-3a25b0113650', 'Theatre Career Management', 'THEA4150', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'F'),
('6a8aa7f8-308d-4a18-8c5e-da2135f4eac6', 'Playwriting II', 'THEA4290', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'W'),
('74be529a-c15c-4e2c-900a-72449951471a', 'Independent Study I', 'THEA4300', 6.0, 'For highly motivated fourth-year Theatre majors who have an Overall GPA of 6.0 or better and a Major GPA of 7.0 or better, and who wish to pursue special and intensive personal study in an independent non-studio course under the supervision of an appropriate Area faculty member. Note: Open by application to the Department of Theatre Undergraduate Education Committee.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('c2a52ef3-e9a4-44b8-bf48-5b5bff364543', 'Independent Study I', 'THEA4300', 3.0, 'For highly motivated fourth-year Theatre majors who have an Overall GPA of 6.0 or better and a Major GPA of 7.0 or better, and who wish to pursue special and intensive personal study in an independent non-studio course under the supervision of an appropriate Area faculty member. Note: Open by application to the Department of Theatre Undergraduate Education Committee.', 'FA', 'W'),
('81bfcd7d-8546-40cd-8b11-140532dc3a8d', 'Independent Study II', 'THEA4301', 3.0, 'For highly motivated fourth-year Theatre majors who have an Overall GPA of 6.0 or better and a Major GPA of 7.0 or better, and who wish to pursue special and intensive personal study in an independent studio course under the supervision of an appropriate Area faculty member. Note: Open by application to the Department of Theatre Undergraduate Education Committee.', 'FA', 'W'),
('7a307ebe-fe17-4aed-96e5-cdced8240587', 'Independent Study II', 'THEA4301', 3.0, 'For highly motivated fourth-year Theatre majors who have an Overall GPA of 6.0 or better and a Major GPA of 7.0 or better, and who wish to pursue special and intensive personal study in an independent studio course under the supervision of an appropriate Area faculty member. Note: Open by application to the Department of Theatre Undergraduate Education Committee.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('4224cb4a-2dc5-4e8c-a94b-a4f69442ac74', 'Performance, Politics, and Human Rights', 'THEA4331', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'F'),
('2c35d532-76e6-4883-b75e-7a84f9b2509f', 'Aspects of Directing', 'THEA4485', 3.0, 'Provides an Investigation and application of the fundamental and intermediate principles of directing for the theatre. Prerequisites: FA/THEA 3051 3.00 or FA/THEA 3210 6.00 or FA/THEA 3290 6.00; FA/THEA 3070 3.00 or FA/THEA 3071 3.00; or by application to the department. Corequisites: FA/THEA 4070 3.00 or FA/THEA 4071 3.00. Course credit exclusion: FA/THEA 4480 6.00.', 'FA', 'F'),
('479d8422-c615-402d-9652-34ed7d05ac7f', 'The Photographic Experience', 'VISA1006', 3.0, 'This fully online course provides non-majors with an introduction to photographic concepts, theories and practices using personal Smartphone cameras. Students participate in online classes comprised of lectures, readings, personal reflections, artist examples, image analysis and photographic assignments. Basic photographic techniques, creative methods, concepts and contemporary modes of mobile image creation are explored. A Smartphone equipped with a camera is required. Students are required to attend live weekly tutorial sessions over Zoom. Students looking for more advanced camera skills using a DSLR or mirrorless camera should enroll in VISA 2061. This course cannot be used as a prerequisite for 3000 level photography courses. Open to non-majors only.', 'FA', 'W'),
('0cc04a3e-8552-4c0a-b317-7213b28e1cf9', 'Art Fundamentals: Concept, Creativity and Production', 'VISA1010', 6.0, 'Introduces students to the visual arts studios, faculty and a spectrum of production methods addressing two dimensional, three dimensional and the element of time in art making. An established theme provides students with a starting point for research, conceptualization and the exploration of fundamental processes as a means of visualizing their creative ideas. Course work is presented at an end of term exhibition.', 'FA', 'F'),
('43e3b97e-5efc-4788-be80-720c6ef982ed', 'Painting Approaches to the Human Figure', 'VISA2021', 3.0, 'Examines Aesthetics and techniques through the study of various traditions and approaches to painting the human figure. Visual vocabulary and formal composition are developed through practical application and critical appraisal. Contemporary art concerns are discussed in slide presentations.', 'FA', 'W'),
('738b423e-a34d-4131-915f-88d142c8442e', 'Creating Space in Contemporary Painting', 'VISA2023', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'W'),
('17a01e20-b120-49cb-8c03-e3c473d1fd24', 'Painting: Composition and Colour', 'VISA2024', 3.0, 'Introduces composition and colour through a number of assigned painting projects on how to structure a painting. Emphasis is on formal elements, particularly colour, and combining them through a diversity of compositional means that cover a range of painting genres. Course credit exclusion: FA/VISA 2022 3.00.', 'FA', 'F'),
('e2aba718-2cd1-4f92-9f4a-f145f4e82e7e', 'Introduction to Sculpture: Soft Sculpture', 'VISA2030', 3.0, 'This Experiential Education course is designed to engage students in creating three-dimensional, non-wearable sculpture in soft materials such as textiles, flexible foam and paper. Through a combination of presentations and hands-on studio projects, this course will explore soft materials as a sculptural medium, and contextualize the use of soft materials in both historical and contemporary frameworks of artistic production, emphasizing individual, process-based approaches to creative problem solving. The sculptural processes surrounding fabrication of soft sculptures introduces students to a range of sculptural methodologies and skills employing pattern drafting, assembly, and material manipulation. Through in-class demonstrations students are introduced to a range of skills and tools to be utilized in the production of their studio projects. Assignments consist of a series of projects exploring different working processes and sculptural methodologies. In addition to these projects, in-class discussions, technical assignments and readings engage students in the dialogue surrounding contemporary sculptural concerns such as form, process and spatial awareness. Craftsmanship and a demonstrated knowledge of materials are a major factor in this course. In order for students to be able to fully articulate their idea in a given material, they must understand the limits and possibilities inherent in the fabrication process. It is expected that each student will develop a degree of sophistication and competence when working with these material processes. Lectures, demonstrations, group critiques and hands-on instruction are the essential means by which we will investigate sculptural problem-solving. The class participates in material specific demonstrations, technical assignments and class critiques of finished work. Periodically there will be in-class work periods, but it is expected that the majority of the assignments will be completed outside of class time.', 'FA', 'W'),
('5f6cad56-8b76-43d9-9042-df1b05e7e809', 'Introduction to Sculpture: Mould Making', 'VISA2033', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'W'),
('a525ee32-fb7c-4b83-b5ab-06da5767b319', 'Introductory Foundry', 'VISA2036', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'F'),
('58a298e0-c872-4531-82e4-42a1988b4008', 'Introduction to Ceramic Sculpture', 'VISA2037', 3.0, 'Designed to expose students to various processes used to develop sculpture in clay. Projects will focus on themes relevant to understanding object materiality, and offers a solid framework of clay and ceramic sculpture techniques, concept development, evaluation skills, and is the foundation for continuing on into Advanced Ceramic Sculpture.', 'FA', 'F'),
('71180870-a70e-4457-b3c6-6e48d02bcb80', 'Time-Based Art: Crossing Boundaries', 'VISA2053', 3.0, 'Introduces students to interdisciplinary studio practice, emphasizing critical thinking and creative exploration of time and process. Sound, video and performance are investigated. Technical workshops include introductions to sound and video editing, audio recorder and microphone use. This course is cross-listed between the Department of Visual Arts and the Theatre Department.', 'FA', 'F'),
('a8e1542e-e29e-49fc-a14b-9ac2c64f9a67', 'Time-Based Art: Performance Art and Everyday Life', 'VISA2055', 3.0, 'Focuses on individual and group projects plus readings on time-based media, especially contemporary performance art. Students participate in exercises that promote original responses to the everyday environment, and are encouraged to use a variety of media in making works. This course is cross-listed between the Department of Visual Arts and the Theatre Department.', 'FA', 'W'),
('2c00bdea-babe-42ec-a715-58dfd4c13475', 'Time-Based Art: Introduction to Video Art - Production', 'VISA2056', 3.0, 'Introduces students to the creative practice of video art in a production studio environment, including both concepts and techniques. Classes include workshops on camera, lighting, video effects and sound recording techniques. Students create individual creative video projects. Compulsory supplementary fees.', 'FA', 'W'),
('4896d5dd-f549-4204-b64d-e2f8384f46d4', 'Photography: Introduction to the Black and White Darkroom', 'VISA2060', 3.0, 'This hands-on studio course is an exploration of B&W processes and darkroom technique in a contemporary context. With a focus on analogue production, students are taught B&W negative and print production, DIY camera creation and camera-less photography techniques. Technical proficiency and aesthetic awareness are nurtured alongside the development of concept-driven artworks. This course has compulsory supplementary fees. Students are required to work on campus. Access to an analogue, manually operable camera is required. Students seeking camera and photography fundamentals should register in VISA 2061. VISA 2060 is open to visual arts majors.', 'FA', 'W'),
('8e7e0be3-f251-4b64-9528-a0d075df0c4c', 'Photography: Digital Fundamentals - Camera to Print', 'VISA2061', 3.0, 'This course offers an introductory studio in photographic theory and practice fundamentals. Tools, techniques and ideas related to seeing and photo-based art are explored with emphasis on the photographer as an informed image-maker. Students gain hands-on skills in camera fundamentals, capture and printing, while developing an understanding of the basic principles of lens-based image making. Short, topic-oriented production assignments enable ongoing critical reflection and analysis. This course has compulsory supplementary fees. Access to a DSLR or mirrorless camera is mandatory and not available through VAAH. Students will require access to a computer equipped with Adobe Photoshop (on-campus computers are available in The Intersections Lab - ROOM 328). Students seeking photographic post-production techniques for the screen & web should register in VISA 2065. Open to visual arts majors and is a prerequisite for 3000-level photography courses.', 'FA', 'F'),
('ed8873b7-9aaa-466a-bfe6-39956fb653a2', 'Photography: Digital Fundamentals - Camera to Print', 'VISA2061', 3.0, 'This course offers an introductory studio in photographic theory and practice fundamentals. Tools, techniques and ideas related to seeing and photo-based art are explored with emphasis on the photographer as an informed image-maker. Students gain hands-on skills in camera fundamentals, capture and printing, while developing an understanding of the basic principles of lens-based image making. Short, topic-oriented production assignments enable ongoing critical reflection and analysis. This course has compulsory supplementary fees. Access to a DSLR or mirrorless camera is mandatory and not available through VAAH. Students will require access to a computer equipped with Adobe Photoshop (on-campus computers are available in The Intersections Lab - ROOM 328). Students seeking photographic post-production techniques for the screen & web should register in VISA 2065. Open to visual arts majors and is a prerequisite for 3000-level photography courses.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('d0c9bc1e-f365-4095-beef-58380896d0e8', 'Photography: Digital Post-Production for Screen and Web', 'VISA2065', 3.0, 'This online course introduces students to photographic software and concepts related to the digital environment. Students will acquire skills in digital workflow and image processing by introducing raw files to Adobe Lightroom and Photoshop. Students will use software to produce and manipulate photographs for the screen & web. By the end of the course students will have acquired a working knowledge of the basic tools, applications and creative techniques related to digital, screen-based photography. This course has compulsory supplementary fees. Students will require access to a computer equipped with Adobe Lightroom Classic and Photoshop. Access to a DSLR camera is advantageous. Requires skill in basic digital photography. Students seeking camera and photography fundamentals should register in VISA 2061 before taking this course. VISA 2065 is open to visual arts majors and is a prerequisite for 3000-level photography courses. This course is cross-listed with FA/FILM 2065 3.00 and is open to Film majors (BA and BFA). Course credit exclusions: FA/VISA 2006 3.00; FA/FILM 2065 3.00.', 'FA', 'W'),
('4d6547c0-fbd4-403e-862c-bc593d94daa0', 'Print Media: Screenprinting', 'VISA2074', 3.0, 'In this introduction to screen-printing, students are exposed to the ideas and aesthetics informing contemporary print media. Demonstrations and projects cover various approaches to screen-printing with a range of techniques and material processes.', 'FA', 'F'),
('6cfb77da-e911-4153-9ed1-ef00c255b5e8', 'Imprints and Monoprints: Print-Based Drawing', 'VISA2075', 3.0, 'In this foundation-level printmaking course, students are exposed to a hybrid of printmaking and print-based drawing processes. Projects explore a range of print and mark-making gestures as they occur both within and outside the studio, utilizing both press-based and hand-based printing. Topics draw from ideas and aesthetics informing contemporary print media, including: the transfer of object to imprinted trace; making and finding; the artist''s hand and the occurrence of chance. Techniques include monoprinting, collagraph and object printing through a general introduction to intaglio and relief print processes. Demonstrations and lectures introduce students to a range of techniques, material processes and thematic content utilized across both historical and contemporary artmaking. Compulsory Supplementary Fees. Open to non-majors.', 'FA', 'W'),
('2dbbffee-8606-495f-b326-75faaa0d9b80', 'Drawing: Perception, Proportion, Structure', 'VISA2081', 3.0, 'This in-person studio-based course introduces students to observational drawing skills, providing hands-on techniques and concepts of representational drawing from a contemporary perspective. Observational methods such as sighting, measuring, analysis of form, space and volume are combined with techniques of rendering such as tonal values, spatial perspective, and composition. This course seeks to examine and develop drawing skills as a way of actively observing, engaging with, and relating to the world around us while simultaneously developing artistic voice and material sensibility.', 'FA', 'F'),
('08b06c50-8873-4d0b-8e57-b440eeb452cd', 'Drawing: Contemporary Practices', 'VISA2083', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'W'),
('b3dba217-ab2b-4f4c-997a-027b7df49944', 'Drawing: Image and Object', 'VISA2084', 3.0, 'An integrated sculpture / drawing course that introduces concepts and techniques of drawing and object making as they relate to translation of object to image, and from image to object. Open to non-majors.', 'FA', 'W'),
('2fa8ae87-1e39-46e2-a23f-a22785dcc787', 'Publishing in Digital Media', 'VISA2100', 3.0, 'Explores content development, publishing and the flow of information across a range of digital platforms that comprise our contemporary communications ecosystem. Topics include visual and digital literacy, a brief history of the Internet, the World Wide Web and the cloud, and our dependence on social media and mobile devices. As opportunities for increased interactivity and data collection are enabled by the proliferation of sensors and pervasive computing, students will consider the efficacy of digital communications to issues of identity, privacy, work, individual well-being, citizenship and the public good. Students will work in multi-media, web development, and social media to complete assignments. Cross-listed with FA/VISA 2100 3.0. Course credit exclusion: FA/FACS 2500 3.0.', 'FA', 'W'),
('ea9dd027-22f5-4c10-8d06-81ad9eccbd5b', 'Introduction to 3D Modelling', 'VISA2210', 3.0, 'Provides a foundation in 3D modelling using state of the art render time 3D modelling software such as Maya, Blender, and 3DS Max. The course will provide a survey of various modelling techniques and approaches with an emphasis on modelling used in 3D art, 3D animation and games. Topics include photorealistic rendering, scene building, character modelling, and the use of 3D graphics in simulation and visualization.', 'FA', 'F'),
('3766dc74-08d6-40f9-8550-c44bc1e6fb7e', 'Introduction to 3D Animation', 'VISA2220', 3.0, 'Provides a foundation in 3D animation using state of the art render time 3D modelling and animation software such as Maya, Blender, and 3DS Max. The course will provide a survey of various animation techniques and approaches with an emphasis on render time animation as it is used in 3D art, 3D animation, data visualization and games. Topics include, scene building, character animation, timeline based animation techniques, and the use of 3D graphics in simulation and visualization. Prerequisites: FA/DATT 2500 3.00, or by permission of the Instructor.', 'FA', 'W'),
('e44d7890-9e17-47b0-98a8-5e29c68084bd', 'Painting: A 2- and 3- Dimensional Practice', 'VISA3024A', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'F'),
('39e5d017-2975-411b-91bf-f05f33881c9e', 'Painting and Mass Media', 'VISA3024B', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'W'),
('bfa8988a-cf59-43af-b7b8-71d954224736', 'Painting: Shifting the Boundaries of Abstraction', 'VISA3024C', 3.0, 'Examines the recent shift of the boundaries of abstraction and figuration in pictorial experience. In a series of painting, students create new relationships between abstraction and figuration by using the visual strategies of convergence, interference and mutation. Prerequisite: FA/VISA 1000 3.00; six credits from the FA/VISA 202x series of courses.', 'FA', 'F'),
('a20aa44b-9ed6-41a3-b884-5b8d5d53b4d7', 'Advanced Ceramic Sculpture', 'VISA3032C', 3.0, 'Explores the highly experimental and immediate process of modeling and firing clay to create sculpture. This course builds upon the information presented in Introduction to Ceramic Sculpture, with the goal of pushing the possibilities of the material, while developing personal research projects in clay. Prerequisite: FA/VISA 2037 3.00.', 'FA', 'W'),
('f4b5bec3-7056-429e-91d9-58991bef3130', 'Intermediate Sculpture Processes: Foundry - Lost wax/Ceramic Shell', 'VISA3032E', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'W'),
('82ecf1c2-d69c-47a1-aa94-16a8fda5b50c', 'Figurative Sculpture', 'VISA3032I', 3.0, 'Introduces students to the possibilities of creating figurative sculpture utilizing various techniques and material processed. Through the completion of assignments, student gain practical knowledge of how to successfully craft sculpture based on the human anatomy. Students experiment with diverse processes, such as casting from the body, modeling in clay, and working in diverse materials in order to gain a personal understanding of the potential and limits of figurative sculpture. In addition to developing skills in constructing figurative sculpture, students are expected to develop a conceptual engagement with representations of the human body. This is achieved through discussing contemporary sculptors whose practices are rooted in the representation of the human figure (such as Evan Penny, Louise Bourgeois, Anthony Gormley). Lectures, demonstrations, group critiques and most importantly, hands-on instruction are the essential means by which we investigate the contemporary figurative sculpture. Prerequisite: three credits from FA/VISA 203x 3.00 series of courses.', 'FA', 'F'),
('23d55c47-1a47-48ef-ba9e-498dd515c77a', 'Modelling for 3D Fabrication', 'VISA3033', 3.0, 'Introduces students to the possibilities for creating digital objects using advanced 3D design software and 3D scanning technologies, and the related conceptual concerns. Prerequisite: three credits from FA/VISA 203x 3.00 series of courses; for DIGM students: FA/DATT 2050 3.00.', 'FA', 'F'),
('f70d007c-6e15-4d1f-a092-f4046ee7fbb2', 'Digital Fabrication', 'VISA3034', 3.0, 'Introduces students to the possibilities for translating digital objects into physical objects using three-dimensional printing technologies, and the related conceptual concerns. Prerequisite: FA/DATT 3940 3.00 or FA/VISA 3033 3.00.', 'FA', 'W'),
('6a85bee0-4ff2-4152-a87e-61fb2ce63c66', 'Hybrid Approaches to Black and White Photography', 'VISA3060', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'F'),
('251adf55-0585-4a1d-8a89-a7cf95bc931a', 'Photography: The Constructed Image', 'VISA3067', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'F'),
('558196ac-1a5b-43c3-801b-427b11b80304', 'Photography: The Constructed Image', 'VISA3067', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'W'),
('5704d409-7ad5-447c-9753-599032e7aa9e', 'Artists'' Books and Multiples', 'VISA3071', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'F'),
('bed0793a-aa9f-4b40-8797-bb0108360ab1', 'Print Media: Advanced Screenprinting and Digital Printing Processes', 'VISA3074', 3.0, 'The study of hybrid printmaking processes focusing on screenprinting and digital printing. Students build upon previous studio work with photo-based screenprinting to develop advanced work in the medium. Compulsory supplementary fees apply. Prerequisite: FA/VISA 2074 3.0', 'FA', 'W'),
('159f7172-1098-4abe-ba0a-5d74765f4c28', 'Drawing Methods', 'VISA3080', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'F'),
('b812e97e-7610-4d8f-a07f-979a8807c06b', 'Interpreting the Model', 'VISA3084', 3.0, NULL, 'FA', 'W'),
('6791251a-3833-4483-9896-5ff98f313d39', 'Image and Narrative', 'VISA3085', 3.0, 'Examines a variety of narrative structures through the drawing process to develop personal imagery and concepts applicable to a broad range of media.', 'FA', 'F'),
('4c684615-204b-40aa-ab02-a2c0e58d1c54', 'Professional Practices for Visual Artists', 'VISA4001', 3.0, 'Introduces upper level Visual Arts Majors to the contemporary art world and the professional practices that they will encounter as emerging visual artists giving them the knowledge and the practical skills to understand the extended art community and to navigate their own diverse paths within it. Course content includes lectures on the structure of art institutions, including artist residencies and how artists can leverage their abilities to navigate this system. The course content will expand student''s understanding of a professional studio practice, encompassing collaborative professional development with other disciplines (science, technology, engineering etc), artist driven curatorial projects, collaborative community based projects, and examining the possibilities for traditional research based practices (working with archives, educational institutions, etc.). Lectures, guest speakers, and field trips address a variety of topics such as an evaluation of graduate programs, grant writing, contracts, copyright / intellectual property, exhibition proposals, research practices and skills, networking, social media promotion, documentation archive, marketing and business development. Students evolve individual plans for transitioning from undergraduate study to post graduate study or work by researching and analyzing the requirements and limitations of opportunities relevant to their current practice and future goals.', 'FA', 'F'),
('617b1fa2-7507-4edd-8b68-afd121da9a6c', 'Honours Studio', 'VISA4090', 6.0, 'VISA 4090 is a capstone studio course designed for BFA Honours students. This course provides senior-level students with the opportunity to develop an independent studio practice, furthering their creative, conceptual and technical skills within both an area specific and interdisciplinary focus. Over the year students are expected to develop a self-directed, cohesive body of work, while making connections between their practice and a broader context of art and ideas. Pre-requisite: Fourth year standing with six credits from the 3000 level studio courses with a grade of B or better.', 'FA', 'Y'),
('6b34bfa0-5cc4-4319-9367-a5f7c367422e', 'Independent Studies: Practicum', 'VISA4091', 3.0, 'Offers an individualized study program for highly motivated visual arts students in their final year of study who wish to pursue specific and intensive independent projects under the supervision of a faculty member on topics not available in other courses in visual arts. Supplementary fees are charged in studio areas where such fees are applicable. Prerequisite: Applications are restricted to students who fulfill all of the following criteria: BFA Honours major in visual arts with a minimum overall average of 7.0 (B+); successful completion of 90 credits towards the BFA, including a minimum of six credits in FA/VISA 4090 6.00 series in the proposed discipline. Note: The annual submission deadline for an independent studies application is by the end of April for the next summer and fall/winter academic sessions. Applications are subject to department approval. Applications are not available after the deadline.', 'FA', 'F'),
('a567c323-5740-4a7d-b884-29373ff2936b', 'Introduction To Financial Accounting I', 'ACTG2010', 3.0, 'This two core course sequence develops students'' understanding of financial accounting information so that they can be informed and effective users of the information. The courses focus on uses of accounting information for different decisions and from different stakeholder perspectives, and consider the economic and behavioural effects that accounting treatments have on users and preparers. Readings from current publications are used to demonstrate practical applications of the issues discussed in class. Classroom techniques such as case studies, classroom discussions, student presentations and group and individual research projects (intended to develop students'' critical skills) are employed. Note: SB/ACTG 2011 3.00 is not available to exchange students visiting Schulich unless it is a full year exchange and SB/ACTG 2010 3.00 is taken in the fall. Course credit exclusions: GL/ECON 2710 3.00, AP/ECON 3580 3.00, AP/ECON 4200 3.00.', 'SB', 'F'),
('8fe484a0-7d53-4415-ab2d-6918581c7e1d', 'Introduction To Financial Actg II', 'ACTG2011', 3.0, 'This two-course sequence develops students'' understanding of financial accounting information so that they can be informed and effective users of the information. The courses focus on uses of accounting information for different decisions and from different stakeholder perspectives, and consider the economic and behavioural effects that accounting treatments have on users and preparers. Readings from current publications are used to demonstrate practical applications of the issues discussed in class. Classroom techniques such as case studies, classroom discussions, student presentations and group and individual research projects (intended to develop students'' critical skills) are employed. Prerequisite: SB/ ACTG 2010 3.00. Course Credit Exclusion: GL/ECON 2710 3.00.', 'SB', 'W'),
('db21c7cc-8727-4067-b50c-bea8516dec36', 'Management Accounting Concepts', 'ACTG2020', 3.0, 'This core course stresses the understanding of basic accounting concepts which underlie management decisions for performance appraisal, pricing, output, financing, investment and other purposes encountered in various organizational settings. Emphasis is placed on applying these concepts in case situations, rather than on technical aspects of management accounting. Note: This course cannot be completed via the MBA program as a Guided Study course. Corequisite: SB/ACTG 2011 3.00. Course Credit Exclusion: GL/ECON 2720 3.00.', 'SB', 'W'),
('9b9bf516-aece-490d-90c8-44ccdc2d17d9', 'Financial Reporting and Analysis', 'ACTG3000', 3.0, 'Publicly-issued financial statements are "general purpose" statements, issued to a variety of users and prepared using generally accepted accounting principles (GAAP). However, the statements are based on management''s financial reporting objectives (e.g. profit maximization; income smoothing; tax minimization), as well as on many management estimates and disclosure decisions. Statements based on GAAP do not usually reflect the needs of the individual user. In this course, students will learn to apply diagnostic, analytical and judgmental skills to understanding financial statements from the point of view of external users who need to make various types of decisions such as performance evaluation, loan decisions, and stock valuation. Note: Not available to exchange students visiting Schulich. Open to Schulich undergraduate students with minimum year 3 academic standing. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 2011 3.00', 'SB', 'F'),
('12b94165-4cea-499d-b65c-cdb35458be23', 'Financial Reporting and Analysis', 'ACTG3000', 3.0, 'Publicly-issued financial statements are "general purpose" statements, issued to a variety of users and prepared using generally accepted accounting principles (GAAP). However, the statements are based on management''s financial reporting objectives (e.g. profit maximization; income smoothing; tax minimization), as well as on many management estimates and disclosure decisions. Statements based on GAAP do not usually reflect the needs of the individual user. In this course, students will learn to apply diagnostic, analytical and judgmental skills to understanding financial statements from the point of view of external users who need to make various types of decisions such as performance evaluation, loan decisions, and stock valuation. Note: Not available to exchange students visiting Schulich. Open to Schulich undergraduate students with minimum year 3 academic standing. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 2011 3.00', 'SB', 'W'),
('9f84178e-ef13-4c35-8f96-9176f8df7b2a', 'Intermediate Financial Accounting I', 'ACTG3110', 3.0, 'Examines the concepts, objectives and techniques underlying asset valuation and income determination, including alternative asset measurement bases and accounting for changing prices. The course has a decision orientation. Special emphasis is placed on accounting policy choices and the criteria by which such choices are made, as well as on analyzing financial statements prepared under differing accounting policy alternatives. Note: Not available to exchange students visiting Schulich. Open to Schulich undergraduate students with minimum Year 3 academic standing. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 2011 3.00', 'SB', 'F'),
('aec13db9-3d4f-4dc0-8950-dea5aae51f37', 'Intermediate Financial Accounting II', 'ACTG3120', 3.0, 'This is an extension of SB/ACTG 3110 3.00, but with a primary focus on the valuation and presentation of liabilities and owners'' equity. Major topics include current, long-term and contingent liabilities; leases; pensions; corporate income tax allocation; capital transactions, earnings per share and analysis of financial statements under differing accounting policies. The criteria by which both preparers and users make decisions are emphasized. Note: Not available to exchange students visiting Schulich. Open to Schulich undergraduate students with minimum Year 3 academic standing. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 3110 3.00.', 'SB', 'F'),
('67fce6b1-8fed-47b5-834a-040f6a67b66f', 'Intermediate Financial Accounting II', 'ACTG3120', 3.0, 'This is an extension of SB/ACTG 3110 3.00, but with a primary focus on the valuation and presentation of liabilities and owners'' equity. Major topics include current, long-term and contingent liabilities; leases; pensions; corporate income tax allocation; capital transactions, earnings per share and analysis of financial statements under differing accounting policies. The criteria by which both preparers and users make decisions are emphasized. Note: Not available to exchange students visiting Schulich. Open to Schulich undergraduate students with minimum Year 3 academic standing. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 3110 3.00.', 'SB', 'W'),
('4a6f1101-d739-463c-8261-ecfb65877a34', 'Advanced Financial Reporting', 'ACTG4150', 3.0, NULL, 'SB', 'W'),
('46546b11-8454-4cdf-9611-913d734b6f82', 'Contemporary Issues In Accounting', 'ACTG4200', 3.0, 'This course concentrates on the application of accounting theory to controversial issues in financial accounting. The topics covered vary with the changing importance of current accounting issues. Source materials include current accounting literature and the research publications of professional accounting organizations. The development of research, writing and analytical skills is emphasized. Note: Not available to exchange students visiting Schulich. Open to Schulich undergraduate students with minimum Year 4 academic standing. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 4600 3.00 and SB/ACTG 4710 3.00', 'SB', 'W'),
('7cff494a-e0bc-4624-b18d-42639434fe2d', 'Managerial Cost Accounting & Analysis', 'ACTG4400', 3.0, 'This course develops problem-solving skills for internal accounting applications. Topics include: cost concepts and analysis, cost accumulation for product costing and variance analysis, and cost analysis for decisions involving alternatives. Cases and problems are used. Note: Not available to exchange students visiting Schulich. Open to Schulich undergraduate students with minimum Year 3 academic standing. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 2020 3.00', 'SB', 'F'),
('5353c538-919c-4752-9c8d-dd2f97ba18d9', 'Managerial Cost Accounting & Analysis', 'ACTG4400', 3.0, 'This course develops problem-solving skills for internal accounting applications. Topics include: cost concepts and analysis, cost accumulation for product costing and variance analysis, and cost analysis for decisions involving alternatives. Cases and problems are used. Note: Not available to exchange students visiting Schulich. Open to Schulich undergraduate students with minimum Year 3 academic standing. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 2020 3.00', 'SB', 'W'),
('658427e3-87b9-4163-8749-8221568bd216', 'Auditing Standards & Applications', 'ACTG4600', 3.0, 'This course focuses on the standards and applications underlying the latest function and the responsibilities of external and internal auditors. The theory of audit evidence and certain basic techniques are used to provide an understanding of auditing methodology. The auditor''s responsibility beyond the financial audit and current developments in auditing are also examined. Students may be expected to complete a research paper or project. Note: Not available to exchange students visiting Schulich. Open to Schulich undergraduate students with minimum Year 4 academic standing. Prerequisites: SB/ACTG 3120 3.00 Students who were on exchange in Year 3 may take SB/ACTG 3120 3.00 and SB/ACTG 4600 3.00 concurrently.', 'SB', 'F'),
('0bea5108-2958-4147-a242-92d4c6c22a4e', 'Introduction To Income Taxation', 'ACTG4710', 3.0, 'The basic concepts and techniques of income taxation and applications to personal and corporate contexts are examined. Emphasis is placed upon accounting applications. Note: Not available to exchange students visiting Schulich. Open to Schulich undergraduate students with minimum Year 4 academic standing. Prerequisites: SB/ACTG 3120 3.00 Students who were on exchange in Year 3 may take SB/ACTG 3120 3.00 and SB/ACTG 4710 3.00 concurrently.', 'SB', 'F'),
('a37cda99-e18d-4b9f-9768-90079db32ea8', 'Advanced Income Taxation', 'ACTG4720', 3.0, 'A continuation of SB/ACTG 4710 3.00, this course concentrates in greater detail on the taxation of business income. Note: Not available to exchange students visiting Schulich. Open to Schulich undergraduate students with minimum Year 4 academic standing. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 4710 3.00.', 'SB', 'W'),
('2fb88c44-fe14-4859-b6e6-40b4ec45d319', 'Guided Study - Selected Problems in Accounting', 'ACTG4900', 3.0, 'N/A', 'SB', 'F'),
('faec3379-6e47-4dc6-94f5-109d0b6863d1', 'Financial Accounting For Managers', 'ACTG5100', 3.0, 'This course examines the assumptions and decision usefulness of financial statements and non-financial information, including MD&A as well as sustainability reports prepared for creditors, shareholders, and stakeholders from society at large. Emphasis is placed on the interpretation of financial statements and the influence of non-financial information, using examples from several companies.', 'SB', 'F2'),
('3b6c8656-55ed-4300-a88f-82e192170c7e', 'Financial Accounting For Managers', 'ACTG5100', 3.0, 'This course examines the assumptions and decision usefulness of financial statements and non-financial information, including MD&A as well as sustainability reports prepared for creditors, shareholders, and stakeholders from society at large. Emphasis is placed on the interpretation of financial statements and the influence of non-financial information, using examples from several companies.', 'SB', 'W2'),
('aa8b80d2-1f50-40f9-964b-35a5e80be742', 'Financial Decisions for Managers', 'ACTG5200', 3.0, 'This course will provide students with an understanding of the financial implications of managers'' decision making and how financial measures can be used to monitor and control business operations. Concepts will include capital budgeting, financial statements, break-even analysis, non-routine decision making, pricing decisions, profit and loss calculation, ROI, and Operating Budget and budgeted financial statements.', 'SB', 'F2'),
('7254557e-6802-4825-912c-bbcbe2f81ad1', 'Management Accounting', 'ACTG5210', 1.5, 'This course provides an introduction to management accounting techniques that are useful in management decision making situations such as: cost management, pricing special orders, determining service levels, performance appraisal, and socially responsible practices. It also considers how a variety of stakeholder interests are incorporated into managerial decision-making processes and reporting. Among these, the course considers the influences and information requirements that feed into the internal and external reporting of various ethical and sustainability practices. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 5100 3.00', 'SB', 'C'),
('908bdc06-638b-42b9-9a46-4bfabdec21f2', 'Management Accounting', 'ACTG5210', 1.5, 'This course provides an introduction to management accounting techniques that are useful in management decision making situations such as: cost management, pricing special orders, determining service levels, performance appraisal, and socially responsible practices. It also considers how a variety of stakeholder interests are incorporated into managerial decision-making processes and reporting. Among these, the course considers the influences and information requirements that feed into the internal and external reporting of various ethical and sustainability practices. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 5100 3.00', 'SB', 'N'),
('8b9800cb-e5d5-488e-84fa-7ad03b12cd21', 'Intermediate Financial Accounting I', 'ACTG6120', 3.0, 'Examines the accounting concepts and principles that relate to the asset accounts. Attention is given to alternative asset measurement bases and related revenue recognition and income determination bases. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 5100 3.00; SB/ACTG 5101 3.00 (previously offered as SB/MACC 5101 3.00) for students pursuing the Schulich Diploma in Intermediate Accounting (DIAc).', 'SB', 'F2'),
('6e44123e-d877-4a2f-948b-56742d8bd664', 'Intermediate Financial Accounting for Finance Majors', 'ACTG6130', 3.0, 'This course is meant specifically for non-accountants who want a broader understanding of accounting issues and how they impact financial statements. This course covers both sides of the balance sheet focusing on understanding the impact of the issues on the financial statements instead of the detailed technical application. This course is not recognized as a course by the accounting designations. Prerequisite: for MBA students SB/ACTG 5100 3.00 or equivalent. Course credit exclusions: SB/ACTG 6120 3.00 and SB/ACTG 6140 3.00 (either SB/ACTG 6130 3.00 or SB/ACTG 6120 3.00 and SB/ACTG 6140 3.00).', 'SB', 'F2'),
('c9b46f26-79cd-4ad1-a2ca-819d86721007', 'Intermediate Financial Accounting II', 'ACTG6140', 3.0, 'This is an extension of SB/ACTG 6120 3.00 with emphasis on the liability of equity accounts. Major topics include: tax allocation, pensions, leases, capital transactions and financial statement analysis. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 6120 3.00.', 'SB', 'F2'),
('dec4b5e4-677d-4c4e-982e-66e1396cf987', 'Intermediate Financial Accounting II', 'ACTG6140', 3.0, 'This is an extension of SB/ACTG 6120 3.00 with emphasis on the liability of equity accounts. Major topics include: tax allocation, pensions, leases, capital transactions and financial statement analysis. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 6120 3.00.', 'SB', 'W2'),
('a72bb7dc-2f6e-413b-9fe6-6c754aff1aee', 'Advanced Financial Accounting', 'ACTG6160', 3.0, 'The final course in the three-course financial accounting core, this course emphasizes accounting for international activities and intercorporate investments. The application of accounting principles to case situations in specialized industries and non-profit organizations is also considered. Prerequisites: SB/ACTG 6120 3.00 & SB/ACTG 6140 3.00. Note: Not available to exchange students visiting Schulich.', 'SB', 'W2'),
('e97ad156-a7d8-471a-aa06-d9b57b686cf3', 'Multi-Competency Case Analysis for Accountants', 'ACTG6201', 1.5, 'This course will build on the case analysis fundamentals learned and applied in the previous terms courses. Focusing on financial accounting, the students will work on integrating their analysis with other competency areas including tax, assurance, management accounting, finance and corporate governance. In addition to the specific competency areas, case analysis considers the impact of the development, implementation and use of information systems for the management and processing of data in business settings. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 6120 3.00 Corequisite: SB/ACTG 6140 3.00 NOTE: Open only to MACC students and MBA students pursuing the CPA designation.', 'SB', 'A'),
('351d3ba2-f355-473e-950b-7fb8aa6d6473', 'Financial Reporting and Analysis', 'ACTG6250', 3.0, 'Examines why stock prices react when firms announce their earnings, and why certain trends in industry earnings affect stock prices of many firms in that industry. Under highly stylised Generally Accepted Accounting Principles (GAAP), abnormal accounting earnings of a firm can be discounted to derive an estimate of the share price. This course therefore offers a direct link between accounting policy choices, abnormal earnings (based on adjusted reported earnings) and stock price valuation. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 5100 3.00. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 5101 3.00 (previously offered as SB/MACC 5101 3.00) for students pursuing the Schulich Diploma in Intermediate Accounting (DIAC). Course Credit Exclusion: SB/FINE 6600 3.00 (either SB/ACTG 6250 3.00 or SB/FINE 6600 3.00, but not both, may be taken for credit).', 'SB', 'F2'),
('05b44201-b19a-4b0a-ac34-f415549ddac7', 'Financial Reporting and Analysis', 'ACTG6250', 3.0, 'Examines why stock prices react when firms announce their earnings, and why certain trends in industry earnings affect stock prices of many firms in that industry. Under highly stylised Generally Accepted Accounting Principles (GAAP), abnormal accounting earnings of a firm can be discounted to derive an estimate of the share price. This course therefore offers a direct link between accounting policy choices, abnormal earnings (based on adjusted reported earnings) and stock price valuation. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 5100 3.00. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 5101 3.00 (previously offered as SB/MACC 5101 3.00) for students pursuing the Schulich Diploma in Intermediate Accounting (DIAC). Course Credit Exclusion: SB/FINE 6600 3.00 (either SB/ACTG 6250 3.00 or SB/FINE 6600 3.00, but not both, may be taken for credit).', 'SB', 'W2'),
('12e36eb7-3494-4f02-beef-359a3caeda6b', 'Integrative Case Analysis for Accountants', 'ACTG6301', 3.0, 'This is the second of six case courses for the MAcc Program This course requires students to integrate knowledge obtained in the courses taken throughout the program and apply that knowledge to cases that incorporate all of the competency areas, including financial reporting, strategy and governance, assurance, finance, management accounting, and taxation. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 6201 1.50 (previously offered as SB/MACC 6201 1.50) NOTE: Open only to MACC students and MBA students pursuing the CPA designation', 'SB', 'W2'),
('64c636d3-9e93-409c-9b2a-9c6eee06a674', 'Advanced Management Accounting', 'ACTG6550', 1.5, 'This course focuses on managerial planning, performance, and control systems in organizations that direct the behaviour of corporate officers and managers, in order to achieve a specific goal. Different tools and techniques are reviewed including methods of incentivizing employee productivity, compliance, and overall performance assessment. The course emphasizes decision-making through the analysis of case studies and discussions. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 5211 3.00 (previously offered as SB/MACC 5211 3.00)', 'SB', 'M'),
('83a1e0b5-d9c5-47cd-b529-4c35cf3f2cb4', 'Auditing Standards & Applications', 'ACTG6600', 3.0, 'This course focuses on the standards and applications underlying the latest functions and responsibilities of external and internal auditors. The theory of audit evidence and certain basic techniques are used to provide an understanding of auditing methodology. The auditor''s responsibility beyond the financial audit and current developments in auditing are also examined. Students may be expected to complete a research paper or project. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 5100 3.00. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 5101 3.00 (previously offered as SB/MACC 5101 3.00) for students pursuing the Schulich Diploma in Intermediate Accounting (DIAC).', 'SB', 'F2'),
('f5ed3b57-2aef-42b4-89eb-69d3a9daa11f', 'Advanced Auditing', 'ACTG6610', 3.0, 'This course extends students'' knowledge in the area of auditing by examining the role of the profession in society today, evaluating current issues facing auditors, and building on their understanding of the general audit framework and its fundamental theories. It also examines specific audit topics such as legal liability, comprehensive auditing, fraud, audit of not-for-profit organizations, environmental auditing and small business audits. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 6600 3.00.', 'SB', 'W2'),
('9ce97872-817f-4b6d-a697-b0ffdfabf23b', 'Introduction to Income Taxation', 'ACTG6710', 3.0, 'The basic concepts and techniques of income taxation and applications to personal and corporate contexts are examined. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 5100 3.00. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 5101 3.00 (previously offered as SB/MACC 5101 3.00) for students pursuing the Schulich Diploma in Intermediate Accounting (DIAC).', 'SB', 'F2'),
('6807ebf8-3571-4000-aa2b-5f187a4f1955', 'Advanced Income Taxation', 'ACTG6720', 3.0, 'A continuation of SB/ACTG 6710 3.00, this course concentrates in greater detail on the taxation of business income. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 6710 3.00.', 'SB', 'W2'),
('6d391000-2c48-4d67-a71e-b16a7eecbf4e', 'Managerial Tax Planning', 'ACTG6730', 3.0, 'This course builds on ACTG 6710 and ACTG 6720. It explores how individual and corporate taxpayers identify and implement tax planning opportunities in an attempt to maximize after-tax incomes, subject to complying with tax laws, while achieving business or financial strategies. Prerequisites: SB/ACTG 6710 3.00, SB/ACTG 6720 3.00.', 'SB', 'W2'),
('5f976cbc-88d4-4920-bc5e-caaed4dbd4e6', 'Individual Study: Accounting', 'ACTG6900', 1.5, 'A student may work with an individual faculty member on a topic of mutual interest. These studies are co-designed by the student and the faculty member based on: 1) learning objectives 2) means of achieving these objectives 3) method of evaluation.', 'SB', 'A'),
('a241845b-054c-4147-9c8b-29e725438f98', 'Individual Study: Accounting', 'ACTG6900', 1.5, 'A student may work with an individual faculty member on a topic of mutual interest. These studies are co-designed by the student and the faculty member based on: 1) learning objectives 2) means of achieving these objectives 3) method of evaluation.', 'SB', 'C'),
('e21e0b4e-e9d0-48a9-9ff2-5fdf772aee51', 'Individual Study - Accounting', 'ACTG6900', 3.0, 'A student may work with an individual faculty member on a topic of mutual interest. These studies are co-designed by the student and the faculty member based on: 1) learning objectives 2) means of achieving these objectives 3) method of evaluation.', 'SB', 'F2'),
('97063040-1a10-456e-a397-9c2cb6d7e220', 'Individual Study - Accounting', 'ACTG6900', 3.0, 'A student may work with an individual faculty member on a topic of mutual interest. These studies are co-designed by the student and the faculty member based on: 1) learning objectives 2) means of achieving these objectives 3) method of evaluation.', 'SB', 'W2'),
('9309f157-90db-458e-b44d-9247c6a46a6d', 'Overview of Accounting Research', 'ACTG7010', 3.0, NULL, 'SB', 'F3'),
('f06db0fe-50ee-4b6b-b5a0-8f08d15f7e8b', 'Advanced Topics in Financial Accounting Research II', 'ACTG7050', 3.0, NULL, 'SB', 'W3'),
('8cc9b152-6d45-471e-bc35-57eaf53058f6', 'Independent Study - Accounting', 'ACTG7900', 3.0, 'N/A', 'SB', 'F3'),
('5303a806-8f68-46e0-bc67-084a3f66ab85', 'Transformation in Media and Culture', 'ARTM6301', 3.0, 'This course will explore the interplay between business, management, politics, and technology within the evolving creative industries globally. It will analyze the influence of emerging ideas, audiences, technologies, and regulations on the creation, funding, distribution, consumption, control, and suppression of media, entertainment, and cultural products, while also assessing their effects on traditional cultural forms.', 'SB', 'W2'),
('f6b0484f-4c86-4199-8edb-c3f605571491', 'Media Models from Network to Netflix', 'ARTM6340', 3.0, 'This course examines innovative business models adapting to digital disruption and reshaping the global media landscape. Topics include content financing, branding, revenue strategies, and evolving consumer demands for interactive content. Drawing on Canadian and global markets, it analyzes streaming''s challenge to traditional media, alongside regulatory and technological impacts, notably Artificial Intelligence. Students gain insights from industry experts. Cross-listed with GS/CMCT 6340 3.00', 'SB', 'F2'),
('8a5f572b-48ad-474e-acfd-ddaea57ead98', 'The Business of Creativity in the Cultural Sector', 'ARTM6350', 3.0, 'Negotiation and management of creative rights processes and people are core to the strategic competence of arts and media organizations and their relationship with audiences. Technological developments are changing the way that these organizations approach their business. This course examines this complex evolving business through such topics as: perspectives on creativity; power, conflict and politics relating to managing unions, free-lance talent and celebrities; decision-making for market-risk; financing models, and the impact of the competitive and regulatory environment on management in the cultural sector. This course will be particularly valuable for those interested in the cultural industries including film, music, publishing, broadcasting and the performing arts. Recommended prerequisite: SB/ARTM 6301 3.00.', 'SB', 'F2'),
('e040a391-efb8-4886-b3fa-df05ecc40206', 'Individual Study: Selected Problems in Arts and Cultural Management', 'ARTM6900', 1.5, 'A student may work with an individual faculty member on a topic of mutual interest. These studies are co-designed by the student and the faculty member based on: 1) learning objectives 2) means of achieving these objectives 3) method of evaluation.', 'SB', 'A'),
