('12ec918f-d623-4669-96b9-902bad2faca7', 'Financial Statement Analysis and Valuation', 'PACC5530', 3.0, 'Students learn to analyse corporate financial performance using financial statements and other information from Canadian companies. They learn multiple methods of valuing companies. They write professional analytical and valuation reports and explain complex technical reports in oral presentations.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('84b598d4-0343-4f39-b43a-1fbc98051053', 'Issues in the Practice of Assurance', 'PACC5550', 3.0, 'The objective of the course is to develop both the technical and practical knowledge necessary to be a competent professional auditor in a variety of engagements. Through writing and presenting professional reports, working in teams, and critiquing current practices, students are guided to provide balanced solutions, which reflect ethical and professional values, to problems that todays professional auditors face.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('b3e7a1ba-36ed-4b39-b4d0-4ab62fadea74', 'Taxation and Financial Decision-Making in Canada', 'PACC5560', 3.0, 'Building on undergraduate courses in personal and corporate tax, this course uses the case study method to help students further develop and integrate the key competencies and tax knowledge needed by Canadian accountants in public practice, industry, and government. It looks at the importance of tax in financial decision-making and financial reporting and good internal control and risk management practices.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('ffdd470f-69b5-4bc3-a2d5-68aa4ee5d543', 'Performance Management Systems', 'PACC5570', 3.0, 'Introduces fundamental theories and concepts behind performance measurements and how these relate to governance, ethics and risk. It makes extensive use of cases to highlight lessons learned and best practices. It aims to develop critical thinking skills via the application of concepts and theories to business cases and improves students understanding of management behavior related to performance measurements. PRIOR TO FALL 2015: Course credit exclusion: GS/FACC 6140 3.00.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('457cfdec-c3ed-4a98-8ce8-ebc9f5af229a', 'Integrative Analysis in Accounting', 'PACC5590', 3.0, 'This case-based course focuses on the development of integrative and analytical skills that professional accountants should possess. Technical areas of study include financial reporting, governance and strategy, management accounting, assurance, finance, and taxation. Through teamwork, report writing, and presentation, students are guided to provide solutions to issues that todays accountants may face, while maintaining ethical behaviour and professionalism.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('69274505-4155-44b6-b32e-2b29bb621614', 'Advanced Methods & Research in the Arts', 'PANF7020', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'S1'),
('84244e24-8469-4641-ba0e-e8f53a50e8c9', 'Crime in the Digital Age: An Overview', 'PCS6167', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'S3'),
('0e175e07-4582-458f-adb5-04b37224925f', 'Crime in the Digital Age: Emerging Risks', 'PCS6168', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'SU'),
('84090df1-6588-4320-833e-a90c536cd4f1', 'Directed Readings', 'PHIL6010A', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'S2'),
('aa7ca288-87f0-483a-a108-381efe1cc571', 'Directed Readings', 'PHIL6010A', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'SU'),
('91bc8fd7-48b5-4705-820d-d9c678e609c6', 'Directed Readings', 'PHIL6010', 3.0, 'In special circumstances, students may take, for credit and in place of regular seminar courses, a reading course directed by a member of the faculty, which should consist of individual tutorial sessions at least once a month. Students must present sufficient written work to form a basis for discussions and for assessment of progress. Directed reading courses are intended to enable students to pursue a research interest not covered by any of the seminar courses offered in that year; to enable them to explore an unfamiliar topic with a view to making it the topic of the thesis or dissertation; or to fill a gap in their knowledge which might impede their special research. M.A. students may not substitute more than one, or Ph.D. students more than two directed readings for regular seminar courses. Directed reading courses are given ex gratia by members of the faculty and the approval of the Program Director must be obtained in advance. This will not normally be given unless the Director is satisfied that the student has the appropriate background to pursue the course at a suitably advanced level.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('389dfc91-6a08-4b0d-b7cd-be3dfb46cbf5', 'Pragmatism', 'PHIL6180', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'S2'),
('b582d0b3-ffa9-423d-a4db-e9337a6c1892', 'Physics Research', 'PHYS5400', 3.0, 'A non-thesis experimental or theoretical research endeavour in physics, supervised by a faculty member. The student and supervising faculty member agree at the outset on the project scope (including required literature review), milestones (including frequency of regular student-faculty meetings), and deliverables (including a final written report).', 'GS', 'SU'),
('792274c1-a281-46b7-8ce0-b633318300a6', 'Astronomical Research', 'PHYS5490', 3.0, 'A non-thesis experimental or theoretical research endeavour in astronomy, supervised by a faculty member. The student and supervising faculty member agree at the outset on the project scope (including required literature review), milestones (including frequency of regular student-faculty meetings), and deliverables (including a final written report).', 'GS', 'SU'),
('30c90deb-cbb6-4446-a83e-89dab11633f5', 'Advanced Topics in Solid State Physics: Solid Mechanics', 'PHYS6100', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'SU'),
('c604f291-cdc3-4bdc-abd1-72e0b3e5c354', 'Advanced Topics in Quantum Electronics - Reading Course', 'PHYS6110', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'SU'),
('7d0d85dc-97a2-4607-b0e6-539dc3ae43b2', 'Selected Topics in Applied Optical Physics', 'PHYS6170', 3.0, 'Topics may change from year to year. Typical subject material may be selected from: design of advanced optical components, instruments and systems, detectors and instruments, the principles of laser radar (lidar), the interaction of laser radiation with materials, optical communication systems, advanced instrumentation for astronomy and space science.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('67fb1c1d-b2ec-42d7-b7e1-09fc8232aca5', 'Selected Topics in Physics', 'PHYS6213', 1.0, 'This course covers a selected topic in theoretical or experimental physics.', 'GS', 'S1'),
('156f4907-f017-4ef1-88fd-74f4c9c51c29', 'Selected Topics in Astronomy', 'PHYS6214', 1.0, 'This course covers a selected topic in theoretical or observational astronomy.', 'GS', 'S1'),
('1c72156e-a69c-4c59-a774-32dcc67d0472', 'International Political Economy and Ecology Summer School', 'POLS6282', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'I2'),
('1d80a878-7c99-41a2-94d1-06ade27b360d', 'Reading Course', 'POLS6990', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'SU'),
('cbdcc361-728a-4771-af37-1387ff3b0490', 'Reading Course', 'POLS6990A', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'SU'),
('928f226f-6c06-4213-83e5-9f1103dcf52a', 'M.A. Major Research Paper', 'POLS6999', 6.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'SU'),
('b71cc238-486c-48ab-8bc3-47dee7915de2', 'Public Finance and Accounting', 'PPAL6020', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'SU'),
('0c07b0da-6df8-4060-8466-3a4e937550b2', 'Equity, Policy, Law and Planning', 'PPAL6130', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'SU'),
('e4933848-fd1d-4c65-aeec-f320c1eb5ed5', 'Internship/Co-Op', 'PPAL6900', 3.0, 'This is the course equivalent for an internship/co-op placement consisting of work in a public or para-public institution related to public policy, administration and law; supervised by a committee of designated faculty members responsible for the academic evaluation. Placement supervisors provide written reports reflecting on the student''s work experience.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('e95652a9-1038-4c2f-8359-2238bf5e4c88', 'Independent Readings and Research', 'PPAL6990', 3.0, 'In exceptional cases, students in the Graduate Program in wishing to gain expertise in a particular area may take up to two three-credit independent reading and research courses with the approval of the graduate program director', 'GS', 'SU'),
('3b8b7e09-aaf9-4536-93a5-6c2eeb5f78e8', 'Clinical Practicum II', 'PSYC6440P', 6.0, 'In this course, students are given practical training in psychodiagnosis and in advanced approaches to psychological intervention. The training is mediated through demonstration, role playing, and supervised management of a small number of clinical cases. Prerequisites: Psychology 6420.06, Psychology 6430.06, Psychology 6435.06 and Psychology 6430P.06.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('3214ec19-6765-4de8-be2a-c585846b83f1', 'Clinical Practicum III (Optional)', 'PSYC6460P', 6.0, 'This practicum provides extra clinical training that the student and the Director of Clinical Training deem necessary for the student to meet training requirements, such as assuring that the student has sufficient training in both assessment and intervention skills. This practicum may be taken only after the Ph.D. III academic year and provided that the student has completed all course work, and either the minor area paper or an approved Ph.D. dissertation proposal.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('06310293-fcd7-4fda-9021-4a243b24b8e5', 'Readings', 'PSYC6710', 6.0, 'A reading course for a student or group of students with a faculty member in any area not covered by the psychology courses offered in a particular year. Note: Enrolments in this course are limited and are authorized for individual students only on the specific recommendation of the Graduate Programme Director. See Program Director for specific procedures involved.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('0d39c797-553f-4fd8-b696-6289feb170b1', 'Readings', 'PSYC6710B', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'SU'),
('ffc1afe6-e9d3-4ab6-90f2-416ae51cdcda', 'Applied Practicum II', 'PSYC6810B', 3.0, 'Supervised experience in the application of the principles of psychology to the amelioration of applied problems.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('00234732-190a-491e-95b5-15d0d4a4d3ad', 'Applied Practicum I', 'PSYC6810A', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'SU'),
('83b7495d-a8fc-4c94-9b85-289397ddcc9e', 'Research Practicum I', 'PSYC6820A', 3.0, 'Supervised experience in the design, execution, analysis and communication of psychological research.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('fca4d908-95e9-4c78-b8f6-4f2a1babfd0e', 'Research Practicum II', 'PSYC6820B', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'SU'),
('6075d005-944b-43b4-9814-ec8d41c6003e', 'Research Practicum III', 'PSYC6820C', 3.0, 'Supervised experience in the design, execution, analysis and communication of psychological research.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('23bc8512-9fce-4c2f-aa75-fb02ff5cf119', 'Research Practicum IV', 'PSYC6820D', 6.0, 'Supervised experience in the design, execution, analysis and communication of psychological research.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('22fc4cab-02cb-4693-b25a-e6570e99163e', 'Clinical Internship II', 'PSYC6840B', 3.0, 'PhD students in the Clinical area and the Clinical-Developmental area complete a 12-month full-time clinical internship or a 24-month part-time clinical internship at the end of the PhD program. Internships reflect a minimum of 1600 hours of training. The half-time internship option requires a minimum of 800 hours of training in each of the two years.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('7478fad6-05f4-4e73-8dd4-88b29412e707', 'Clinical Internship', 'PSYC6840', 6.0, 'PhD students in the Clinical area and the Clinical-Developmental area complete a 12-month full-time clinical internship or a 24-month part-time clinical internship at the end of the PhD program. Internships require a minimum of 1600 hours of training.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('e3f93a3b-ee93-4637-8d66-38a391c89b3c', 'Biological and Cognitive Bases of Development', 'PSYC6905', 3.0, 'This course will focus on the psycho-neuropathhology and biological origins of various childhood behaviour disorders. For these purposes, information will be drawn from clinical and experimental neuropsychology, ethology and comparative psychology, and developmental neurobiology and genetics.', 'GS', 'S1'),
('678815a8-57aa-49aa-ba86-fd1fd64c4992', 'Diversity Issues in Children, Youth and Adults in Clinical Practice', 'PSYC6965', 1.5, NULL, 'GS', 'S1'),
('1018c2ed-875e-4092-8793-af700583f16c', 'Investment Funds, Hedge Funds & Private Equity', 'SELA6128', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'S3'),
('58d2d1c0-201f-482b-b816-c5b5a2689586', 'Selected Topics in Securities Law', 'SELA6701', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'S3'),
('320ead90-51e9-400b-bcff-74e20ce5df4b', 'Directed Reading', 'SLST5900', 3.0, 'Students may take ONE half-course reading course as part of their Master of Arts'' studies with the permission of the graduate program director and the Instructor.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('8856d594-a936-4fae-ab29-3ccb496cf86c', 'Interviewing Methods', 'SOCI6095', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'S1'),
('961ee745-d2d4-49b0-ba2f-8abb37605ac2', 'Transformative Approaches in Mental Health', 'SOWK5912', 3.0, 'This course explores issues related to the psychiatric and mental health system from critical perspectives. Students are introduced to dominant policy, legislative and practice discourses on "mental health" and "mental illness" and their critiques. Counter-discourses and approaches to dominant ''psy'' discourses, systems, and practices on "mental distress" are captured through an exploration of community mobilization and community-based advocacy and support networks.', 'GS', 'S1'),
('eee34b93-225b-4fbb-8ef2-b1283af972a9', 'Group Facilitation and Social Justice', 'SOWK5915', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'S1'),
('42ed863b-6595-4915-b7e7-66247ee954dc', 'Selected Topics in Social Policy and Social Work: Family Mediation', 'SOWK5950B', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'S1'),
('b882f331-e3bf-4df4-b36d-21d8e2b0cf56', 'Changing Nature of Community Work', 'SOWK5952', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'S1'),
('612d9752-fd53-4a53-b36a-9c2ae1260442', 'Directed Readings', 'SOWK5970', 3.0, 'Individual students or small groups will read under supervision in one or two selected areas. Students wishing to enrol are to contact the Director of the Graduate Program in Social Work for approval.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('0428e95c-cb57-40be-80d5-6c0a5c854935', 'Directed Readings', 'SOWK5970B', 3.0, 'Individual students or small groups will read under supervision in one or two selected areas. Students wishing to enrol are to contact the Director of the Graduate Program in Social Work for approval.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('6be4531f-874e-4237-9985-39aeea5677ca', 'Violence in Families', 'SOWK5980', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'S1'),
('93add57f-37bc-447b-b6c9-18e50f2f7926', 'Narrative Therapy and Critical Social Work', 'SOWK5983', 3.0, 'This course explores the possibilities and limits of using Narrative therapy as a critical social work practice. Students explore through experiential learning how narrative ideas may lend themselves to addressing social justice in every day practice with individuals, groups and larger communities. Prerequisite or corequisite: GS/SOWK 5150 3.00.', 'GS', 'S1'),
('b3e9b798-9bbe-49bb-a182-d2f20b08451e', 'Directed Readings', 'SPTH6001', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'SU'),
('33efbd78-b4d1-4d53-8364-06021abbaeb6', 'Directed Reading', 'SPTH6001B', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'SU'),
('d9e5a768-eff4-4096-8632-db6a998f45b0', 'Social and Political Thought: Theories, Approaches, and Methods II', 'SPTH6107', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'S1'),
('1d6b4526-dcb6-4bd4-8419-6a71b8756583', 'Directed Readings for M.A. Students', 'STS5000', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'SU'),
('1983bdf4-5019-4e1c-a45f-0a5c49b9e983', 'Directed Readings for Ph.D. Students', 'STS6000', 3.0, 'N/A', 'GS', 'SU'),
('a0e637d6-9e3a-46b3-a3b0-a57fcc5bf78d', 'Theatre, Dance, & Performance Studies Placement', 'THST5051', 3.0, 'This course is designed to give graduate students applied, professionally-oriented work experience in a field related to one of the program''s fields of specialization and/or the student''s research areas. Note: This course is graded on a pass/fail basis.', 'GS', 'S2'),
('859447b7-9540-46c1-8ea0-dac502d59a81', 'Theatre, Dance, & Performance Studies Placement', 'THST5051', 3.0, 'This course is designed to give graduate students applied, professionally-oriented work experience in a field related to one of the program''s fields of specialization and/or the student''s research areas. Note: This course is graded on a pass/fail basis.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('5d2fbd34-63e8-436f-8092-ae8b61096f3f', 'Independent Studies', 'THST6500', 6.0, 'This course is an opportunity for advanced research and in-depth reading in advanced aspects of theatre studies including but not limited to postcolonial theatre and drama, theory and performance studies and theatre, health and social change.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('08cf8e3d-8a2c-4a0d-b9f9-20e76bc1424b', 'Independent Studies', 'THST6500A', 3.0, 'This course is an opportunity for advanced research and in-depth reading in advanced aspects of theatre studies including but not limited to postcolonial theatre and drama, theory and performance studies and theatre, health and social change.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('554fb4ae-01d6-4690-8f08-fb22d195b104', 'Independent Studies', 'THST6500B', 3.0, 'This course is an opportunity for advanced research and in-depth reading in areas related to students'' research interests. Requests for an Independent Study course must be accompanied with an Independent Study Proposal (with the permission of the Graduate Program Director).', 'GS', 'S2'),
('8fc0822f-b482-4349-9535-8b94a2c390db', 'Desire In Translation: Explorations in Contemporary Theories and Practices', 'TRAS5240', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'SU'),
('99eb9b4b-c4af-425c-a174-3aa50e00a79f', 'Traffic in / of Meaning: Intersections of Travel Writing and Translation', 'TRAS5801', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'SU'),
('1b2b830a-1854-401d-a96a-0b51ff0361ea', 'Reading Course/Travail Individuel', 'TRAS5900', 3.0, 'Special reading courses may be arranged with individual faculty members subject to the approval of the Director of the Graduate Program. Reading courses are designed to enable a student to pursue a research interest or acquire special skills which are not covered by available courses.', 'GS', 'SU'),
('a5ed97ad-f654-4e41-9411-7a0769977011', 'Taxation of Trusts and Estates', 'TXLW6731', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'S3'),
('d017d8ff-d1ee-4be7-83e4-fb95e6190caf', 'Solving Advanced Corporate Tax Problems', 'TXLW6837', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'SU'),
('43de84a3-6ef0-451a-b9b6-fddba1f6fec4', 'Global Eco-Arts Residency', 'VISA5030', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'SU'),
('8e363512-3e6e-4911-b88d-43159868cea9', 'Contemporary Visual Arts Summer Institute', 'VISA6020', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'SU'),
('1589ba69-4ee4-4f75-8016-497ea01d05d4', 'Contemporary Visual Arts Summer Institute', 'VISA6030', 3.0, NULL, 'GS', 'SU'),
('57203c74-e474-43d5-bac5-851f46802ce6', 'Directed Reading', 'VISA6300', 3.0, 'A supervised reading course on a topic for which there is no current course offering. Permission of the Graduate Program Director is required', 'GS', 'SU'),
('051d6e6b-8369-45a5-a846-1d916f1db037', 'Introduction To Financial Accounting I', 'ACTG2010', 3.0, 'This two core course sequence develops students'' understanding of financial accounting information so that they can be informed and effective users of the information. The courses focus on uses of accounting information for different decisions and from different stakeholder perspectives, and consider the economic and behavioural effects that accounting treatments have on users and preparers. Readings from current publications are used to demonstrate practical applications of the issues discussed in class. Classroom techniques such as case studies, classroom discussions, student presentations and group and individual research projects (intended to develop students'' critical skills) are employed. Note: SB/ACTG 2011 3.00 is not available to exchange students visiting Schulich unless it is a full year exchange and SB/ACTG 2010 3.00 is taken in the fall. Course credit exclusions: GL/ECON 2710 3.00, AP/ECON 3580 3.00, AP/ECON 4200 3.00.', 'SB', 'S1'),
('d1f27a99-884c-423c-aa77-6de9fbbe8a1e', 'Introduction To Financial Actg II', 'ACTG2011', 3.0, 'This two-course sequence develops students'' understanding of financial accounting information so that they can be informed and effective users of the information. The courses focus on uses of accounting information for different decisions and from different stakeholder perspectives, and consider the economic and behavioural effects that accounting treatments have on users and preparers. Readings from current publications are used to demonstrate practical applications of the issues discussed in class. Classroom techniques such as case studies, classroom discussions, student presentations and group and individual research projects (intended to develop students'' critical skills) are employed. Prerequisite: SB/ ACTG 2010 3.00. Course Credit Exclusion: GL/ECON 2710 3.00.', 'SB', 'S2'),
('925b33c9-062a-451f-8a10-383fb0057b1e', 'Intermediate Financial Accounting I', 'ACTG3110', 3.0, 'Examines the concepts, objectives and techniques underlying asset valuation and income determination, including alternative asset measurement bases and accounting for changing prices. The course has a decision orientation. Special emphasis is placed on accounting policy choices and the criteria by which such choices are made, as well as on analyzing financial statements prepared under differing accounting policy alternatives. Note: Not available to exchange students visiting Schulich. Open to Schulich undergraduate students with minimum Year 3 academic standing. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 2011 3.00', 'SB', 'S2'),
('4cb62f67-5e18-4460-be2d-19a810abf23a', 'Advanced Financial Reporting', 'ACTG4150', 3.0, NULL, 'SB', 'SU'),
('95e1994d-4e7d-4db2-b442-ed2b3d471539', 'Financial Accounting Fundamentals', 'ACTG5101', 3.0, NULL, 'SB', 'H1'),
('85ea861f-5613-4f47-94b7-47108f1f221a', 'Financial Decisions for Managers', 'ACTG5200', 3.0, 'This course will provide students with an understanding of the financial implications of managers'' decision making and how financial measures can be used to monitor and control business operations. Concepts will include capital budgeting, financial statements, break-even analysis, non-routine decision making, pricing decisions, profit and loss calculation, ROI, and Operating Budget and budgeted financial statements.', 'SB', 'S'),
('4ec84a6e-3cae-474d-a8ad-7d123942bd7f', 'Management Accounting', 'ACTG5210', 1.5, 'This course provides an introduction to management accounting techniques that are useful in management decision making situations such as: cost management, pricing special orders, determining service levels, performance appraisal, and socially responsible practices. It also considers how a variety of stakeholder interests are incorporated into managerial decision-making processes and reporting. Among these, the course considers the influences and information requirements that feed into the internal and external reporting of various ethical and sustainability practices. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 5100 3.00', 'SB', 'E'),
('7e5857db-ac0a-4aa3-9da4-b7213f0c93a3', 'Management Accounting Fundamentals', 'ACTG5211', 3.0, NULL, 'SB', 'H2'),
('060244c3-99ad-4220-a6c9-e12ce11ea552', 'Intermediate Financial Accounting I', 'ACTG6120', 3.0, 'Examines the accounting concepts and principles that relate to the asset accounts. Attention is given to alternative asset measurement bases and related revenue recognition and income determination bases. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 5100 3.00; SB/ACTG 5101 3.00 (previously offered as SB/MACC 5101 3.00) for students pursuing the Schulich Diploma in Intermediate Accounting (DIAc).', 'SB', 'H2'),
('b2a6ed61-e786-4639-a88c-73692c3c90b5', 'Advanced Financial Accounting', 'ACTG6160', 3.0, 'The final course in the three-course financial accounting core, this course emphasizes accounting for international activities and intercorporate investments. The application of accounting principles to case situations in specialized industries and non-profit organizations is also considered. Prerequisites: SB/ACTG 6120 3.00 & SB/ACTG 6140 3.00. Note: Not available to exchange students visiting Schulich.', 'SB', 'SA'),
('8994b229-14f6-40fc-a334-8a65458b6ee3', 'Integrative Case Analysis for Accountants', 'ACTG6301', 3.0, 'This is the second of six case courses for the MAcc Program This course requires students to integrate knowledge obtained in the courses taken throughout the program and apply that knowledge to cases that incorporate all of the competency areas, including financial reporting, strategy and governance, assurance, finance, management accounting, and taxation. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 6201 1.50 (previously offered as SB/MACC 6201 1.50) NOTE: Open only to MACC students and MBA students pursuing the CPA designation', 'SB', 'SA'),
('391dc8db-84ce-4ac2-bc1c-cb7f29ead394', 'Advanced Integrative Case Analysis', 'ACTG6401', 3.0, NULL, 'SB', 'Q7'),
('0938b18b-0ad1-4d42-a7f1-07c3498a2754', 'Integrative Case Analysis: The Capstone', 'ACTG6501', 3.0, NULL, 'SB', 'Q8'),
('db33ff56-159d-460a-bee3-743c973a01e4', 'Management Accounting: Strategy & Performance', 'ACTG6601', 3.0, NULL, 'SB', 'Q5'),
('9a925c78-b306-4b96-b22d-66fdf374bdf0', 'Advanced Auditing', 'ACTG6610', 3.0, 'This course extends students'' knowledge in the area of auditing by examining the role of the profession in society today, evaluating current issues facing auditors, and building on their understanding of the general audit framework and its fundamental theories. It also examines specific audit topics such as legal liability, comprehensive auditing, fraud, audit of not-for-profit organizations, environmental auditing and small business audits. Prerequisite: SB/ACTG 6600 3.00.', 'SB', 'SA'),
('a8e5a4ed-c31d-456c-99aa-2d05825f8458', 'Strategic Performance Evaluation', 'ACTG6650', 3.0, NULL, 'SB', 'SA'),
('086357de-1ce9-4e77-8395-15bf389909a6', 'Managerial Tax Planning', 'ACTG6730', 3.0, 'This course builds on ACTG 6710 and ACTG 6720. It explores how individual and corporate taxpayers identify and implement tax planning opportunities in an attempt to maximize after-tax incomes, subject to complying with tax laws, while achieving business or financial strategies. Prerequisites: SB/ACTG 6710 3.00, SB/ACTG 6720 3.00.', 'SB', 'SA'),
('b963c18d-e457-4973-a96b-2a52ce68122b', 'Strategic Leadership Planning and Case Analysis', 'ACTG6801', 3.0, NULL, 'SB', 'Q6'),
('8fb55f82-d2a6-4c25-a355-63970a344e55', 'Individual Study: Accounting', 'ACTG6900', 1.5, 'A student may work with an individual faculty member on a topic of mutual interest. These studies are co-designed by the student and the faculty member based on: 1) learning objectives 2) means of achieving these objectives 3) method of evaluation.', 'SB', 'E'),
('7917487e-72d3-4c2c-8ebc-21aa972ea3ec', 'Individual Study - Accounting', 'ACTG6900', 3.0, 'A student may work with an individual faculty member on a topic of mutual interest. These studies are co-designed by the student and the faculty member based on: 1) learning objectives 2) means of achieving these objectives 3) method of evaluation.', 'SB', 'S'),
('5ab40cb6-eac7-491b-9389-b221b9b128e2', 'Individual Study: Selected Problems in Arts and Cultural Management', 'ARTM6900', 1.5, 'A student may work with an individual faculty member on a topic of mutual interest. These studies are co-designed by the student and the faculty member based on: 1) learning objectives 2) means of achieving these objectives 3) method of evaluation.', 'SB', 'E'),
('9360531a-5e4f-4ba9-a763-aef884115498', 'Individual Study: Selected Problems in Economics', 'ECON6900', 1.5, 'A student may work with an individual faculty member on a topic of mutual interest. These studies are co-designed by the student and the faculty member based on: 1) learning objectives 2) means of achieving these objectives 3) method of evaluation.', 'SB', 'E'),
('233c17c8-e107-4f2b-8aac-82aad873f5be', 'Individual Study: Selected Problems in Economics', 'ECON6900', 3.0, 'A student may work with an individual faculty member on a topic of mutual interest. These studies are co-designed by the student and the faculty member based on: 1) learning objectives 2) means of achieving these objectives 3) method of evaluation.', 'SB', 'S'),
('b4c989f5-908a-4023-8a1f-512b9749bc54', 'Competitive Strategy', 'EMBA5050', 2.0, NULL, 'SB', 'ES'),
('fad504c4-bb8c-4206-aa02-29186498823c', 'Marketing', 'EMBA5180', 2.0, 'This course helps students to understand contemporary marketing practices based on traditional transactional perspectives versus contemporary relationship and network perspectives. Key concepts and controversies in marketing are explored by comparing practices in contrasting contexts such as business-to-consumer versus business-to- business markets. The impacts of globalization, technological developments, socio-cultural changes and high velocity competition on the emergence and implementation of marketing strategy are examined.', 'SB', 'ES'),
('70070390-e256-4936-b4fa-ebffd88dc2a3', 'Financial Management', 'EMBA5220', 2.0, NULL, 'SB', 'ES'),
('4db6212c-2aaf-42a3-ab12-775758f55da6', 'Operations Management', 'EMBA5460', 2.0, NULL, 'SB', 'ES'),
('74f3e3ed-be60-4860-8d92-917e2434631e', 'Building an AI Powered Organization', 'EMBA6018', 2.0, NULL, 'SB', 'ES'),
('fa76a836-dc0d-48a5-8519-6688f133a50b', 'Sustainable Finance', 'EMBA6027', 2.0, NULL, 'SB', 'ES'),
('a22e3dbb-7abb-49d3-a9a6-02cd74a827e5', 'Sustainable Functions: Driving Organizational Success', 'EMBA6028', 2.0, NULL, 'SB', 'ES'),
('eff1363b-6476-4a58-b987-f4422509493d', 'People Oriented Leadership', 'EMBA6031', 2.0, NULL, 'SB', 'ES'),
('845f1c7b-14a2-4ea8-bebc-bcd118247688', 'Authentic Leadership Journey', 'EMBA6085', 2.0, NULL, 'SB', 'ET'),
('8051ff97-9016-4d8f-bb32-263dd0ff5e1b', 'Strategic Decisions in Operations', 'EMBA6110', 2.0, NULL, 'SB', 'ES'),
('b2d90b2a-8e52-407d-8f11-b68ded1fa320', 'Marketing Analytics', 'EMBA6170', 2.0, NULL, 'SB', 'ES'),
('e8006741-2cc0-4661-a345-84e9d14b1ecf', 'Future Proofing Brands', 'EMBA6245', 2.0, 'In this course, students will learn four methodologies that will give them systematic tools and roadmaps to future proof brands: (i) Voice of the Product, (ii) Blue Ocean Strategy, (iii) Jobs to Be Done, and (iv) Profit Models. It will be taught in a blended way as part of a two-course module together with EMBA 6540 Designing Brand Experiences.', 'SB', 'ES'),
('7051d106-99e4-418c-b5d8-504e8ec80ab7', 'Leveraging Collaboration for Innovation', 'EMBA6265', 2.0, NULL, 'SB', 'ES'),
('9dfbcc2b-9ce2-4e54-86ab-1302a9b73192', 'Strategic Crisis Management', 'EMBA6340', 2.0, NULL, 'SB', 'ES'),
('b5d6e488-8a85-4112-8f31-78be4a464635', 'Negotiations', 'EMBA6400', 2.0, NULL, 'SB', 'ES'),
('3b796e07-99ea-4454-ab04-679b3c31b0b6', 'Responsible Business Leadership', 'EMBA6455', 2.0, 'This course introduces students to five fundamental strategies companies use to respond to growing pressures to adopt more responsible business practices. Through these strategies, students will develop an understanding of the major causes that leave companies at odds with society and how leadership in responsible business requires graduates to challenge the fundamental institutions that guide industrial activity.', 'SB', 'EM'),
('dd39b1b7-1781-49d1-8c25-ea49c5308893', 'Designing Brand Experiences (Blended Course)', 'EMBA6540', 2.0, 'Focusing on the branded corporation as a value creating system, this course aims to develop (a) an integrative managerial approach and mind-set for designing compelling and distinctive brand experiences in todays hyper-competitive marketplace, and (b) the knowledge and perspective necessary to lead, facilitate and support the design and implementation of desired brand experiences and to continuously enhance them over time.', 'SB', 'ES'),
('0a444465-69b8-4c8a-bc73-719e5f14e819', 'Entrepreneurial Finance', 'EMBA6670', 2.0, NULL, 'SB', 'ES'),
('4601a249-7547-42c6-8c7e-f318d47e875e', 'Entrepreneurship and New Firm Creation', 'ENTR6605', 3.0, 'Explores various dimensions of the creation of new ventures, including family enterprises. It is concerned with content and process questions as well as with formulation and implementation issues that relate to conceptualizing, developing and managing successful new ventures. The course examines the nature of entrepreneurs and what they do. It identifies the nature of opportunities and considers how new venture ideas can successfully be screened. The course provides the opportunity to develop a cohesive and affective business plan for a start-up venture.', 'SB', 'S'),
('000e89e2-ae02-49c3-868f-76c2e7094c16', 'Startup Marketing', 'ENTR6610', 3.0, NULL, 'SB', 'S'),
('773d03af-1472-496c-9136-530a1933128d', 'Designing Startup Communities: Inside Toronto''s Innovation Ecosystem', 'ENTR6630', 3.0, NULL, 'SB', 'K'),
('cae87f9e-b3a1-4d90-9808-5c3ac8e44ec3', 'Individual Study: Selected Problems in Entrepreneurial Studies', 'ENTR6900', 1.5, 'A student may work with an individual faculty member on a topic of mutual interest. These studies are co-designed by the student and the faculty member based on: 1) learning objectives 2) means of achieving these objectives 3) method of evaluation.', 'SB', 'E'),
